hadiths[358]{hadithnumber,text}:
"13389","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The pleasure of the Lord, Blessed and Exalted, is in the pleasure of the father, and the anger of the Lord, Blessed and Exalted, is in the anger of the father.""**"
"13390","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever wishes that his lifespan be extended for him and his provision increased, let him be dutiful to his parents and join his kinship ties.""**"
"13391","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Obedience to Allah is obedience to the father, and disobedience to Allah is disobedience to the father.""**"
"13392","Muadh ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever is dutiful to his parents, glad tidings to him, Allah will extend his life.""**"
"13393","Rafi ibn Makith, may Allah be pleased with him, who was among those present at Hudaybiyah, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Dutifulness is an increase in lifespan, and charity prevents an evil death.""**"
"13394","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said that he had carried his mother on his neck for two farsakhs in severe heat, heat so intense that a piece of meat cast into it would have cooked, and asked whether he had repaid her gratitude, to which the Prophet ﷺ replied: > **""Perhaps it equals a single pang of birth.""**"
"13395","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a man was carrying his mother while performing tawaf and asked the Prophet ﷺ whether he had fulfilled her right, to which he ﷺ replied, or words to that effect: > **""No, not even for a single pang.""**"
"13396","Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ accompanied by an old man, and the Prophet ﷺ asked him who was with him, and when the man replied that it was his father, he ﷺ instructed him: > **""Do not walk ahead of him, do not sit before him, do not call him by his name, and do not expose him to abuse.""**"
"13397","Abu Ghassan al-Dabbi reports that he was once walking with his father on the stony lava ground when Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, met him and asked who the man was, and when he replied that it was his father, Abu Hurayrah counseled him on the rights of a father and then spoke of a disobedient man's punishment: > **""Do not walk in front of your father, but behind him or beside him. Never let anyone come between you and him. Never walk on a rooftop with your father beneath it. Never eat a marrow bone toward which your father has looked, for perhaps he desires it.""**"
"13398","Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a man from Yemen emigrated to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him whether he had abandoned polytheism but reminded him that jihad remained, then asked whether his parents were in Yemen, and when he said yes, asked whether they had given him permission, and when he said no, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded: > **""Return to your parents, seek their permission, and if they grant it then proceed, otherwise remain dutiful to them.""**"
"13399","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said that he longed for jihad but was unable to undertake it, and the Prophet ﷺ asked whether any of his parents remained, and when he replied that his mother remained, he ﷺ instructed him to seek Allah's approval through dutifulness to her, for doing so would earn the reward of a pilgrim, one performing umrah, and a warrior, and when his mother became pleased with him he should remain Godfearing and dutiful to her: > **""Seek Allah's acceptance through dutifulness to her, for when you do so you shall have the reward of a pilgrim, one performing umrah, and a warrior in Allah's path. When your mother is pleased with you, then fear Allah and be dutiful to her.""**"
"13400","Muawiyah ibn Jahimah reports from his father, may Allah be pleased with them, that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seeking counsel about jihad, and the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he had parents, and when he said yes, he ﷺ commanded: > **""Stay with them, for Paradise is beneath their feet.""**"
"13401","Talhah ibn Muawiyah al-Sulami, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and said that he wished to strive in Allah's path, and the Prophet ﷺ asked whether his mother was alive, and when he said yes, the Prophet ﷺ commanded: > **""Stay at her foot, for there lies Paradise.""**"
"13402","Nuaym, the freedman of Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, set out for Hajj and, between Makkah and Madinah, recognized a tree and sat beneath it, then related that he had seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ beneath that tree when a young man approached from a mountain pass and stood before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying he had come to strive alongside him in Allah's path seeking Allah's Countenance and the Hereafter, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ asked whether both his parents were alive, and when he said yes, commanded him to return and be dutiful to them, so the man turned back the way he had come: > **""Are both your parents alive? Then return and be dutiful to them.""**"
"13403","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Be dutiful to your fathers, your sons will be dutiful to you, and preserve your chastity, your women will remain chaste.""**"
"13404","Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said, the full narration being recorded in the chapter of apology in the book of manners: > **""Preserve your chastity, your women will remain chaste, and be dutiful to your fathers, your sons will be dutiful to you.""**"
"13405","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the Farewell Pilgrimage, and the first words the Prophet ﷺ uttered were, after which the narrator related the full hadith: > **""Allah, Mighty and Majestic, enjoins upon you kindness toward your mothers.""**"
"13406","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a woman came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked whom she should honor most, and he ﷺ answered her mother, and when she asked who next he again answered her mother, and when she asked a third time he again answered her mother, and when she asked a fourth time he answered her father: > **""Your mother. Your mother. Your mother. Then your father.""**"
"13407","Abdullah ibn Said, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said he had a wife, a mother, and a father, and asked which of them most deserved his kindness, to which the Prophet ﷺ replied: > **""Your mother and your father, your sister and your brother, then the nearest, then the nearest.""**"
"13408","Usamah ibn Sharik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the Farewell Pilgrimage while he ﷺ was saying: > **""Your mother, your father, your sister, your brother, then the nearest, then the nearest.""**"
"13409","Jabir ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ ascended the pulpit and said Amin, Amin, Amin, then explained that Jibril, peace be upon him, had come to him and said that whoever finds one of his parents and dies entering the Fire, may Allah distance him, and bade him say Amin, so he said Amin, then said whoever reaches Ramadan and dies unforgiven entering the Fire, may Allah distance him, and bade him say Amin, so he said Amin, then said whoever hears mention of the Prophet ﷺ and does not invoke blessings upon him and dies entering the Fire, may Allah distance him, and bade him say Amin, so he said Amin: > **""Whoever finds one of his parents and dies entering the Fire, may Allah distance him, say Amin. Whoever reaches the month of Ramadan and dies unforgiven and enters the Fire, may Allah distance him, say Amin. And whoever is reminded of me and does not invoke blessings upon me, then dies and enters the Fire, may Allah distance him, say Amin.""**"
"13410","Malik ibn Amr al-Qushayri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak of freeing a Muslim slave, of one who reaches his parents yet remains unforgiven, and, in another chain, of sheltering an orphan: > **""Whoever frees a Muslim slave, it shall be his ransom from the Fire, and whoever reaches one of his parents and is then not forgiven, may Allah distance him. Any Muslim who embraces an orphan of two Muslim parents into his food and drink until he is independent, Paradise becomes binding for him without fail.""**"
"13411","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that three men of former peoples set out seeking provision for their families when rain seized them, so they entered a cave and a protruding rock fell upon them sealing the opening so that they could see no crevice, and they agreed that the rock had fallen and traces were erased and none knew their place but Allah, so each should supplicate Allah through his most trusted deed. One of them invoked Allah by his practice of milking for his parents into their vessel and, finding them asleep, standing over them unwilling to disturb their rest until they awoke of themselves, done hoping for Allah's mercy and fearing His punishment. The second invoked Allah by his hiring a laborer, rebuking him in anger so that the man left his wage, which he then gathered and invested until it grew into great wealth, all of which he later handed over despite being able to give only the original wage, done hoping for mercy and fearing punishment. The third invoked Allah by his sparing a woman he desired, preserving her chastity and still handing her the payment he had assigned her, done hoping for mercy and fearing punishment. After each supplication part of the rock shifted, until it cleared and they emerged walking arm in arm: > **""O Allah, if You know that I did that only hoping for Your mercy and fearing Your punishment, then relieve us.""**"
"13412","Al-Numan ibn Bashir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mention the Inscription, saying that three men were in a cave when a mountain rock fell upon its entrance and sealed them in, so they bade one another recall a good deed that Allah in His mercy might show them mercy. One recalled that he had hired laborers at fixed wages, then hired a man at midday on the terms of the others, and when that man labored in the rest of the day as much as the others had in the whole day, he deemed it his duty of honor not to reduce him, provoking another worker's protest, to which he replied that he had not wronged him and it was his own wealth to dispose of as he wished, whereupon the worker left in anger abandoning his wage, which he set aside in the house until, by Allah's will, he bought a young cow with it that multiplied greatly, and when the aged owner later returned reminding him of his right, he handed over all of it, refusing to mock him and affirming it was all his right. A second recalled that he had surplus wealth when hardship struck the people, and a woman came seeking kindness, and he refused her unless for herself, and she left and returned and adjured him by Allah, and he still refused, and she told her husband who told her to give herself to him to feed her household, so she returned and implored him by Allah, and still he refused until she yielded herself, but when he unveiled her and was about to commit the act she trembled beneath him saying she feared Allah, Lord of the worlds, so he asked how she feared Him in hardship while he had not feared Him in ease, and left her giving her what he owed for having unveiled her. The third recalled that he had two aged parents and sheep, feeding and watering his parents before returning to his flock, until one evening his pasture took him far and he came late finding them asleep, so he stood with the milk vessel at their heads through the night unwilling to wake them, until dawn broke and they awoke and drank. After each supplication the mountain split further until they saw light and the way became clear: > **""O Allah, if You know that I did that for Your Countenance, then relieve us.""**"
"13413","Al-Numan ibn Bashir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that three men were walking in rain when they passed a cave and took shelter in it, whereupon a rock from the mountain, among those that fall from fear of Allah, blocked the cave, so they agreed that nothing would avail them but that each supplicate through the best deed he had ever done. One of them, a farmer with hired hands, recalled that among them was a man who worked like two men yet he gave him the same wage as the rest, provoking the man's anger and departure abandoning his wage, which he then sowed separately so that it multiplied again and again until the grain became great heaps, and when the needy man later came asking his wage he directed him to those heaps as his wage, and when accused of wronging and mocking him he denied mockery, so the man took them all, and he asked Allah, if He knew he had done that from fear of Him and seeking His Countenance, to uncover them. The rock then shifted leaving a great opening, and the narrator related the rest of the hadith as in the preceding narrations: > **""O Allah, if You know that I did that from fear of You and seeking Your Countenance, then uncover us.""**"
"13414","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that three men set out toward their families when rain seized them and they took refuge in a cave, whereupon a rock fell at its mouth sealing it tight, so they agreed that traces were erased and none knew their place but Allah, and each should supplicate through his most trusted deed done for Allah, hoping He would bring them out. The first invoked Allah by his dutifulness to his parents, describing how he would bring his sheep home at night and milk for his parents and carry it to them lying on their bed to give them drink with his own hand, and how one night he found them asleep yet remained standing at their heads through the night unwilling to wake them and unwilling to return with the drink lest they awake and miss him, until morning came, done seeking Allah's Countenance. The second invoked Allah by his intense love for his cousin, whom he sought in marriage from her family but was refused, until he offered her what she accepted between them, then summoned her and sat from her as a man sits from a woman, but when she said it was not lawful for him to break the seal except by right, he withdrew preserving her honor and person, done seeking Allah's Countenance. The third invoked Allah by a worker employed for a measure of food who left without taking it, remaining away a long age, during which he cultivated that measure until it yielded much cattle, sheep, and wealth, and when the worker returned after a time asking only for the measure of food, he told him all that wealth had come from it and bade him take it all, and when accused of mockery swore by Allah it was the truth, so the man drove away all the wealth. At each supplication the rock split further, until it burst open and they walked out: > **""O Allah, if You know that I did that seeking Your Countenance, then relieve us.""**"
"13415","Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ narrated the account of three men who took shelter in a mountain cave when a rock fell upon them, and how each supplicated through a finest deed — the first through abandoning a forbidden companionship, the second through safeguarding and multiplying a worker's wage until he handed over all its growth, and the third through standing the night with milk at the heads of his two aged needy parents — until the mountain split and they emerged: > **""O Allah, if You know that I did that from fear of You and seeking Your pleasure, then relieve us.""**"
"13416","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that while they were seated with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a youth appeared from a mountain pass, and when he drew near they said among themselves that if only this youth had devoted his strength and youth to Allah's path, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard their remark and replied that Allah's path was not confined to one slain, for whoever strives for his parents is in Allah's path, while whoever strives merely to amass wealth is in the path of false tyranny, with the added wording that whoever strives for his household is in Allah's path: > **""Is there none in Allah's path except one who is slain? Whoever strives for his parents is in Allah's path, and whoever strives for his household is in Allah's path, while whoever strives to amass wealth is in the path of tyranny.""**"
"13417","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that Qutaylah bint Abd al-Uzza, whom Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, had divorced in the pre-Islamic age, sent to her daughter Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, gifts containing dried cheese and clarified butter, but Asma refused to accept her gift or admit her into her house, so she sent word to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, to ask the Prophet ﷺ, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ instructed that she admit her into her house and accept her gift, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed the verse that Allah does not forbid kindness toward those who have not fought you over religion: > **""Let her admit her into her house and accept her gift. Allah does not forbid you those who have not fought you over religion.""**"
"13418","Aishah and Asma, may Allah be pleased with them both, report that their mother came to them in Madinah while she was a polytheist, during the truce between Quraysh and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and they asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether, as she had come with affection, they should maintain ties with her, to which he ﷺ replied affirmatively: > **""Yes, maintain ties with her.""**"
"13419","Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that they were discussing dutifulness before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he began narrating to them of a devout hermit of former nations called Jurayj, who had a wife — or a mother — who would come calling him while he looked down to speak with her, until one day she came while he was absorbed in prayer, and she called out lifting her head and placing her hand upon her forehead, repeating O Jurayj three times, while he kept saying O Lord, my mother or my prayer, until she grew angry and prayed that Jurayj would not die until he had gazed upon the faces of prostitutes. Then the daughter of the village chief became pregnant and bore a boy, and when asked who had done this and who his companion was, she claimed it was Jurayj of the hermitage. Jurayj soon heard axes at the base of his cell, and when he asked what was wrong they did not answer, so he took a rope and descended, whereupon they dragged him by the nose and struck him calling him a hypocrite deceiving people with his deeds. When he protested they accused him of impregnating the chief's daughter, which he denied, and when told she had borne a boy and asked if the boy lived, they said yes, so he bade them stand away, prayed two rakahs, walked to a tree, took a twig, came to the infant in his cradle, struck him with the twig and demanded O tyrant, who is your father, to which the infant replied that his father was such-and-such a shepherd. They then offered to rebuild his cell in gold or silver, but he bade them restore it as it was. Abu Harb added that none spoke in the cradle except three: Isa son of Maryam, the witness of Yusuf, and the companion of Jurayj: > **""O tyrant, who is your father? Restore it as it was.""**"
"13420","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that among the Children of Israel was a man called Jurayj who worshipped in his hermitage when his mother came one day calling O Jurayj, O my son, look down to me that I may speak with you, while he debated O Lord, my mother or my prayer, and continued his prayer, and she returned calling repeatedly, until she prayed that Allah not cause him to die until He had shown him a prostitute. A shepherdess grazing her family's sheep would shelter in the shade of his cell, then committed immorality and became pregnant, and when seized — for among them one who committed adultery was executed — and asked by whom, she named Jurayj of the hermitage. They came with axes and picks bidding him, O hypocrite, descend, but he refused continuing in prayer, so they set about demolishing his cell, and when he saw that he descended, whereupon they bound his neck and hers with a rope and paraded them among the people. He then placed his finger upon her belly and demanded O so-and-so, who is your father, to which the infant answered that his father was such-and-such a shepherd of sheep. They kissed him and offered to rebuild his cell in gold and silver, but he bade them restore it in clay as it had been: > **""O Lord, my mother or my prayer? O so-and-so, who is your father? Restore it in clay as it was.""**"
"13421","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that there was a trader among the Children of Israel who would sometimes lose and sometimes gain, until he declared there was no good in this trade and he would seek a trade better than it, so he built a hermitage and devoted himself therein, after which the narrator related the like of the well-known account of Jurayj: > **""There is no good in this trade; I shall seek a trade better than it.""**"
"13422","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah named them the dutiful because they were dutiful to fathers, mothers, and children; just as your parents have a right over you, so too does your child have a right.""**"
"13423","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs Hajj on behalf of his parents or settles a debt for them, Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection among the dutiful.""**"
"13424","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Help your children toward dutifulness; whoever wishes may extract disobedience from his child.""**"
"13425","Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fulfills their oaths, settles their debts, and does not cause them to be reviled shall be recorded as dutiful even if he was disobedient in their lifetime, and whoever does not fulfill their oaths, nor settle their debts, and causes them to be reviled shall be recorded as disobedient even if he was dutiful in their lifetime.""**"
"13426","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Part of dutifulness is that you maintain ties with your father's friend.""**"
"13427","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Preserve your father's affection, do not sever it, lest Allah extinguish your light.""**"
"13428","Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""He has not been dutiful to his father who fixes his gaze upon him in anger.""**"
"13429","Amr ibn Murrah al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and testified that there is no god but Allah and that he ﷺ is Allah's Messenger, that he prayed the five prayers, paid zakah on his wealth, and fasted Ramadan, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ raised two fingers and replied: > **""Whoever dies upon this shall be with the prophets, the truthful, and the martyrs on the Day of Resurrection thus, so long as he is not disobedient to his parents.""**"
"13430","Maqil ibn Yasar, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah dislikes three things for you: disobedience to mothers, burying daughters alive, and withholding and demanding.""**"
"13431","Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Three have had Paradise forbidden to them by Allah: the habitual drinker of wine, the disobedient to parents, and the shameless one who condones indecency in his household.""**"
"13432","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah will not look upon three on the Day of Resurrection — the disobedient to his parents, the habitual drinker, and the one who reminds others of his gifts — and three shall not enter Paradise — the disobedient to his parents, the shameless one, and the masculinized woman, with a variant wording describing her as the woman who assumes masculine mannerism imitating men: > **""Three whom Allah will not look upon on the Day of Resurrection: the disobedient to his parents, the habitual drinker, and the one who reminds others of his gifts. And three who shall not enter Paradise: the disobedient to his parents, the shameless one, and the woman who imitates men.""**"
"13433","Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that they were with the Prophet ﷺ when a visitor announced that a youth was dying, and when told to say there is no god but Allah he could not, so the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he used to pray and was told yes, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose with his companions and entered upon the youth and bade him declare there is no god but Allah, but he replied he was unable, and when asked why it was explained that he had been disobedient to his parents. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether his mother was alive, and when told yes bade them summon her, and when she came asked whether this was her son, and when she confirmed it, asked her whether, if a great fire were kindled and she were told that her intercession would spare him while otherwise he would be burned, she would intercede, and when she declared she would, bade her call Allah and him to witness that she was pleased with him, so she declared before Allah and His Messenger that she was pleased with her son. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then bade the boy declare that there is no god but Allah alone without partner and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger, which he uttered, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praised Allah who had saved him through him from the Fire: > **""Bear Allah and me witness that you are pleased with him. O boy, say: there is no god but Allah alone without partner, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. Praise be to Allah who has saved him through me from the Fire.""**"
"13434","Abu Ghassan al-Dabbi reports that he was walking with his father on the lava ground when Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, met him and asked who the man was, and when he replied it was his father, Abu Hurayrah bade him not walk ahead of him but behind him or beside him, never to let anyone come between them, never to walk above a rooftop with his father beneath, and never to eat what his father had looked upon lest his father have desired it. Abu Hurayrah then asked whether he knew Abdullah ibn Khidash, and when he said no, related a statement he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about that man's punishment in Hell and the reason for it: > **""His cheek in Hell shall be like Uhud, and his molar like al-Bayda, for he was disobedient to his parents.""**"
"13435","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The fragrance of Paradise is perceived from a journey of five hundred years, yet the one who reminds others of his deeds, the disobedient to parents, and the habitual drinker shall not find its scent.""**"
"13436","Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to them while they were gathered and addressed the Muslims bidding them fear Allah and join their kinship ties, for no reward comes swifter than that of joining kinship, and warning them against disobedience to parents, for the fragrance of Paradise is found from a journey of a thousand years yet the disobedient, the severer of kinship, the oppressor, the aged adulterer, and the one who drags his lower garment in arrogance shall not find it, for grandeur belongs to Allah alone, Lord of the worlds. He added that all falsehood is sin except what benefits a believer or averts a sin, and that in Paradise there is a market with neither buying nor selling but only images, so whoever loves an image of a man or woman shall enter into it: > **""O assembly of Muslims, fear Allah and join your kinship ties, for no reward is swifter than joining kinship. Beware disobedience to parents, for the fragrance of Paradise is found from a journey of a thousand years, yet the disobedient, the severer of kinship, the oppressor, the aged adulterer, and the arrogant trailer of his garment shall not find it. Grandeur belongs only to Allah, Lord of the worlds.""**"
"13437","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever claims another than his father or attaches himself to other than his patrons, turning away from them, upon him is the curse of Allah. So too for whoever reviles his parents, whoever slaughters for other than Allah, whoever violates anything of the sanctity of Makkah, and whoever attributes to me what I did not say.""**"
"13438","Kulayb al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, who had companionship of the Prophet ﷺ, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The eldest among brothers stands in the place of the father.""**"
"13439","Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Three shall cling to the Throne on the Day of Resurrection: kinship saying, O Allah, I am through You, so let me not be severed; trust saying, O Allah, I am through You, so let me not be betrayed; and blessing saying, O Allah, I am through You, so let me not be denied.""**"
"13440","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying that the womb is an offshoot of the Most Merciful, pleading O Lord I have been severed, O Lord I have been mistreated, O Lord I have been wronged, O Lord, O Lord, to which He replies asking whether it would please it that He join whoever joins it and sever whoever severs it: > **""Would you not be pleased that I join whoever joins you and sever whoever severs you?""**"
"13441","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrates that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The womb is an offshoot grasping the waist of the Most Merciful, Mighty and Majestic; He joins whoever joins it and severs whoever severs it.""**"
"13442","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The womb shall be set down on the Day of Resurrection with a hook like the hook of a spindle, speaking with a fluent eloquent tongue, joining whoever joined it and severing whoever severed it.""**"
"13443","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The womb is suspended from the Throne.""**"
"13444","Said ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Among the worst forms of usury is unjustly attacking the honor of a Muslim. This kinship is an offshoot of the Most Merciful, Mighty and Majestic, so whoever severs it, Allah shall forbid him Paradise.""**"
"13445","Amir ibn Rabiah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah, Blessed and Exalted, declared that the womb is an offshoot from Him, so whoever joins it He shall join him, and whoever severs it He shall sever him: > **""The womb is an offshoot from Me; whoever joins it I shall join him, and whoever severs it I shall sever him.""**"
"13446","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The womb is an offshoot grasping the waist of the Most Merciful, pleading its right with Him, and He says: Would you not be pleased that I join whoever joins you and sever whoever severs you? Whoever joins you has joined Me, and whoever severs you has cut himself from Me.""**"
"13447","Jarir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah wrote in the Mother of the Book before creating the heavens and the earth: I am the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; I created the womb and derived for it a name from My names, so whoever joins it I shall join him, and whoever severs it I shall sever him.""**"
"13448","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that the womb is a hooked hold clinging to the Throne, speaking with an eloquent tongue, praying O Allah join whoever joins me and sever whoever severs me, whereupon Allah, Blessed and Exalted, declares that He is the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, having derived for the womb a name from His name, so whoever joins it He shall join him, and whoever tears it He shall tear him: > **""O Allah, join whoever joins me and sever whoever severs me. I am the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; I derived for the womb a name from My name, so whoever joins it I shall join him, and whoever tears it I shall tear him.""**"
"13449","Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The womb shall call out on the Day of Resurrection: whoever joined me, Allah shall join him, and whoever severed me, Allah shall sever him.""**"
"13450","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: > **""The deeds of the children of Adam are presented every Thursday on the eve of Friday, and the deed of one who severs kinship shall not be accepted.""**"
"13451","Al-Amash reports that Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, would sit in a circle after dawn prayer and adjure by Allah any severer of kinship to rise from among them, explaining that they intended to supplicate their Lord while the gates of heaven remained closed before one who severs kinship: > **""I adjure by Allah every severer of kinship to rise from us, for we intend to supplicate our Lord, while the gates of heaven are shut before one who severs kinship.""**"
"13452","Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The angels do not descend upon a people among whom is one who severs kinship.""**"
"13453","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a sermon urging the joining of kinship ties."
"13454","A man of Khatham reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was among his Companions and asked whether he was the one claiming to be Allah's Messenger, and when he ﷺ affirmed it, he asked in turn about the most beloved deeds to Allah and then the most hateful, to which the Prophet ﷺ answered each with three matters: > **""Faith in Allah, then joining kinship, then enjoining good and forbidding evil. Associating partners with Allah, then severing kinship, then enjoining evil and forbidding good.""**"
"13455","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ saying: > **""Charity and joining kinship, through them Allah extends life, repels an evil death, and through them Allah repels the hateful and the dreaded.""**"
"13456","Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no sin more deserves that Allah hasten its punishment in this world along with what He stores for its doer in the Hereafter than severing kinship, treachery, and lying, while the swiftest good in reward is joining kinship, such that a household may be poor yet its wealth grows and its numbers multiply when they remain connected: > **""There is no sin more deserving that Allah hasten punishment for its doer along with what He stores for him in the Hereafter than severing kinship, treachery, and lying. The swiftest good in reward is joining kinship, such that a household may be poor, yet its wealth grows and its numbers multiply when they remain connected.""**"
"13457","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah gives life to dwellings through a people and grants them fruitful wealth though He has not looked upon them with love since creating them, and when asked how that could be, explained it was through their joining their kinship ties: > **""Allah gives life to dwellings through a people and grants them fruitful wealth, though He has not looked upon them with love since creating them, through their joining their kinship ties.""**"
"13458","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no household that remains connected but Allah pours provision upon them, and they dwell under Allah's shelter.""**"
"13459","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Moisten your kinship ties, even with greeting.""**"
"13460","Abu al-Tufayl, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Join your kinship ties through greeting.""**"
"13461","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Learn of your lineages what you may join your kinship ties with.""**"
"13462","Al-Ala ibn Kharijah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Learn of your lineages what you may join your kinship ties with, for joining kinship is love for the family, increase for wealth, and extension of lifespan.""**"
"13463","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The swiftest obedience in reward is joining kinship; even a household steeped in sin shall see its wealth grow and its numbers multiply when they join their kinship ties.""**"
"13464","Amr ibn Sahl (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak about maintaining family ties and its effect on wealth, affection within the household, and the term of life. > **""Maintaining kinship ties brings increase in wealth, love among the family, and postponement of one's appointed term.""**"
"13465","Ali ibn Abi Talib (radiya Allahu anhu) reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning whoever desires a longer life, ample provision, and protection from an evil death, and what such a person should observe toward Allah and kin. > **""Whoever is pleased that his lifespan be extended, his provision be widened, and an evil death be warded off from him, let him fear Allah and maintain his ties of kinship.""**"
"13466","Aishah (radiya Allahu anha) reports that the Prophet ﷺ addressed her concerning the share of gentleness, the good of this world and the Hereafter, and the effect of kinship, good neighbourliness, and good character upon homes and lifespans. > **""Whoever is given his share of gentleness has been given his share of the good of this world and the Hereafter, and maintaining kinship, good neighbourliness, and good character populate homes and increase lifespans.""**"
"13467","Abdullah ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning what stands written in the Torah about loving increase in lifespan and provision and what such a person should do toward kin. > **""It is written in the Torah: whoever loves that his lifespan be increased and his provision be increased, let him maintain his ties of kinship.""**"
"13468","Abu al-Darda (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that kinship was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so those present asked whether one who maintains his kinship has his term extended, and the Prophet ﷺ answered by denying an addition to the lifespan itself, reciting that when their term comes they neither delay it by an hour nor advance it, and then explaining the matter through righteous offspring who supplicate for him after him so that it reaches him, and that this is what is meant by extension. > **""It is not an addition to his lifespan. Allah says: When their term comes, they neither delay it by an hour nor advance it. Rather it is that a man has righteous offspring who supplicate for him after him, and that reaches him, and that is what extends his term.""**"
"13469","Abdullah ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that Quraysh were struck by a severe famine until they ate worn-out bones, and none among Quraysh were more comfortable than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ therefore proposed to Abbas that they go to Abu Talib, whose dependents were many, and take over some of his household. Both went to him and asked to relieve him of some of his dependents, and Abu Talib told them to leave Aqil for him and do as they wished with the rest. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took Ali and Abbas took Jafar, and both boys remained with them until they became independent. Sulayman ibn Dawud added that Jafar remained with Abbas until he emigrated to the land of Abyssinia. > **""O uncle, your brother Abu Talib has many dependents, and Quraysh has been struck by what you see, so come with me to him so that we may take over some of his dependents.""**"
"13470","Jabir (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that Juwayriyah (radiya Allahu anha) told the Prophet ﷺ that she wished to free a certain slave boy, and the Prophet ﷺ instructed her instead to confer him upon a maternal uncle of hers among the bedouins who would put him to work, explaining that this would be greater for her reward. > **""Give him to your uncle among the bedouins, who will put him to pasture work, for that will be greater for your reward.""**"
"13471","Abdullah ibn Amr (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and complained that he had relatives toward whom he maintained ties while they severed him, he pardoned while they wronged him, and he did good while they did evil, and he asked whether he should requite them in kind. The Prophet ﷺ forbade that course, warned of abandonment for all together, and enjoined excellence and continued kinship, giving glad tidings of a supporting angel from Allah remaining with him so long as he persisted. > **""No, for then you would all be abandoned together. Rather take to excellence and maintain ties with them, for there shall remain with you a supporting angel from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, so long as you remain upon that.""**"
"13472","Abu Dharr (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that his close friend ﷺ enjoined him not to let the blame of any blamer overtake him concerning Allah, and further enjoined him to maintain kinship even if the relative turns his back, within a longer narration which the narrator then recounted in full. > **""Let not the blame of any blamer seize you concerning Allah, and maintain your ties of kinship even if the other turns away.""**"
"13473","Abu Hurayrah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned three qualities found in a man for whom Allah would decree an easy reckoning and admit him into Paradise by His mercy. When those present asked what they were, offering their fathers and mothers as ransom, the Prophet ﷺ identified them as giving, joining, and pardoning, and promised Paradise by His mercy for whoever acted upon them. > **""You give to whoever deprived you, you join whoever severed you, and you pardon whoever wronged you, and when you do that, He will admit you into Paradise by His mercy.""**"
"13474","Jarir ibn Abdullah al-Bajali (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned concerning any kinsman who comes to his kinsman and asks him for surplus wealth which Allah has granted him, only for the latter to withhold it out of miserliness, and what Allah would bring forth for him on the Day of Resurrection from Hell as a coiling serpent called Shuja that would be placed about his neck. > **""There is no kinsman who comes to his kinsman and asks him for surplus which Allah has granted him, and he withholds it from him out of miserliness, except that Allah will bring forth for him on the Day of Resurrection a serpent from Hell called Shuja, coiling and watchful, with which he shall be collared.""**"
"13475","Abdullah ibn Amr (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned concerning any man to whom his paternal cousin comes asking for surplus and he refuses him, and what Allah would withhold from him on the Day of Resurrection, in a fuller narration found in the book of transactions. > **""Any man to whom his cousin comes asking him from his surplus and he withholds it from him, Allah will withhold His bounty from him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"13476","Abbas (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed him concerning his children, describing their character and noting for whom their goodness is greatest. > **""O uncle, your children are a disputatious people, and the best of them is for the most distant.""**"
"13477","Abdullah ibn Umar (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning every tree having fruit and the fruit of the heart being the child, concerning mercy toward one's child, an oath by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that none but the merciful shall enter Paradise, and, when those present said that each of them showed mercy, a clarification that mercy here means mercy toward people at large rather than merely toward one's companion. > **""Every tree has its fruit, and the fruit of the heart is the child. Allah does not show mercy to whoever does not show mercy to his child. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, none shall enter Paradise except the merciful. Mercy is not that one of you shows mercy to his companion; rather mercy is that he shows mercy to people.""**"
"13478","Abu Said (radiya Allahu anhu) reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the child as the fruit of the heart and its effect of rendering a man cowardly, miserly, and sorrowful. > **""The child is the fruit of the heart, and indeed it renders one cowardly, miserly, and sorrowful.""**"
"13479","Al-Ashath ibn Qays (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among the delegation of Kindah, and the Prophet ﷺ asked him whether he had children. He replied that a son had been born to him from the daughter of Jamad while he was setting out, and that he would have preferred enough to feed the people to satiety in his place. The Prophet ﷺ forbade him that remark, affirmed the coolness of the eye and the reward when children are taken, and then repeated that children render one cowardly and sorrowful. > **""Do not say that, for among them is coolness of the eye and reward when they are taken. Yet if you say that, they do render one cowardly and sorrowful; indeed they render one cowardly and sorrowful.""**"
"13480","Al-Aswad ibn Khalaf (radiya Allahu anhu) reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he took Hasan, kissed him, then turned to those present and described the effect of children upon a man. > **""Indeed the child renders one miserly, ignorant, and cowardly.""**"
"13481","Abdullah ibn Amr (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivering the sermon upon the pulpit when Husayn ibn Ali (radiya Allahu anhu) came out with a cloth about his neck which he was dragging, tripped over it, and fell upon his face. The Prophet ﷺ therefore descended from the pulpit toward him, and when the people saw this they seized the boy and brought him to him. He took him up, carried him, invoked Allah against Satan, described the child as a trial, and swore that he had not realized his descent until the boy was brought to him. > **""May Allah destroy Satan. Indeed the child is a trial. By Allah, by Allah, I did not realize that I had descended from the pulpit until he was brought to me.""**"
"13482","Abdullah ibn Umar (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning a boy born within a household and the honour that settles among them which had not been there before. > **""No boy is born within a household except that honour settles among them which had not been there.""**"
"13483","Anas ibn Malik (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described what Allah sends at the birth of a girl and of a boy: an angel conveying blessing and announcing the weak come forth from the weak with aid for her guardian until the Day of Resurrection, and an angel from heaven kissing the boy between his eyes and conveying the greeting of Allah. > **""When a girl is born, Allah sends an angel to her conveying blessing in abundance, saying: a weak one come forth from a weak one; her guardian shall be aided until the Day of Resurrection. And when a boy is born, Allah sends an angel to him from heaven who kisses him between his eyes and says: Allah conveys His greeting to you.""**"
"13484","Nubayt ibn Sharith (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described what Allah sends when a daughter is born to a man: angels greeting the household, shading her with their wings, passing their hands over her head, and announcing the weak come forth from the weak with aid for her guardian until the Day of Resurrection. > **""When a daughter is born to a man, Allah sends angels saying: peace be upon you, O people of the house. They shade her with their wings and pass their hands over her head, saying: a weak one come forth from a weak one; her guardian shall be aided until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"13485","Uqbah ibn Amir (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade hatred of daughters and described them as affectionate and precious. > **""Do not hate daughters, for they are affectionate and precious.""**"
"13486","Al-Saib ibn Yazid (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Prophet ﷺ kissed Hasan, whereupon al-Aqra ibn Habis remarked that ten children had been born to him and he had never kissed any of them, and the Prophet ﷺ answered concerning Allah's mercy toward whoever shows no mercy to people. > **""Allah does not show mercy to whoever does not show mercy to people.""**"
"13487","Abdullah ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning a father who looks at his child with delight and the reward of freeing a soul recorded for him. When asked whether this applied even to three hundred and sixty looks, the Prophet ﷺ magnified Allah in reply. > **""When a father looks at his child and it delights him, there is for the father the freeing of a soul. Allah is Greatest.""**"
"13488","Abdullah ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the scent of a child as being from the scent of Paradise. > **""The fragrance of the child is from the fragrance of Paradise.""**"
"13489","Anas (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that a man sat with the Prophet ﷺ when a son of his came, so he kissed him and seated him upon his thigh, and then a daughter of his came and he seated her before him, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him why he had not treated them equally. > **""Why have you not treated them both equally?""**"
"13490","Al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah al-Makhzumi reports that he visited Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ (radiya Allahu anha), who offered to narrate what she had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he assented, she related his words concerning whoever spends upon two daughters, two sisters, or two female relatives, seeking reward, until Allah enriches them from His bounty or suffices them, and what the two would become for him against the Fire. > **""Whoever spends upon two daughters, two sisters, or two female relatives, seeking reward for his spending upon them, until Allah enriches them from His bounty or suffices them, they shall be a veil for him from the Fire.""**"
"13491","Jabir ibn Abdullah (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever has three daughters whom he shelters, treats mercifully, and maintains, and what becomes binding for him without exception. When asked about two, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed the same, and some of the people present judged that had one been asked about, he would likewise have affirmed it. In another wording the marrying off of them is added. > **""Whoever has three daughters whom he shelters, treats mercifully, maintains, and marries off, Paradise becomes binding for him without exception, and likewise if they are two.""**"
"13492","Abdullah ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning whoever has two sisters and keeps excellent companionship with them for as long as they remain with him, and what the two become as a means for him into Paradise. > **""Whoever has two sisters and keeps excellent companionship with them for as long as they remain with him, he shall enter Paradise through them.""**"
"13493","Abu Hurayrah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever sponsors an orphan, whether related or unrelated, and his closeness to him in Paradise with his two fingers joined, and concerning whoever strives upon three daughters and his place in Paradise with the reward of a striving, fasting, standing warrior in the path of Allah. > **""Whoever sponsors an orphan, whether related or unrelated, he and I shall be in Paradise like these two, and whoever strives upon three daughters shall be in Paradise, with for him the reward of a warrior in the path of Allah, fasting and standing.""**"
"13494","Awf ibn Malik (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning any Muslim with three daughters upon whom he spends until they come of age or pass away, and what they become for him against the Fire. When a woman asked about two, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed the same. > **""There is no Muslim with three daughters upon whom he spends until they come of age or pass away, except that they shall be a veil for him from the Fire, and likewise two.""**"
"13495","Anas ibn Malik (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning any of his community with three daughters or three sisters whom he maintains until they come of age, and his companionship with him in Paradise, joining his index and middle fingers in illustration. > **""There is none of my community with three daughters or three sisters whom he maintains until they come of age, except that he shall be with me in Paradise like this.""**"
"13496","Abu al-Muhabbirah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever maintains two daughters, two sisters, two maternal aunts, two paternal aunts, or two grandmothers, and his companionship with him in Paradise like two joined fingers, joining his index and its neighbour in illustration. He then described the burden when they are three, and urged the servants of Allah to reach such a man with loans and partnership when they are four or five, repeating the call to partnership. > **""Whoever maintains two daughters, two sisters, two maternal aunts, two paternal aunts, or two grandmothers, he shall be with me in Paradise like these two. If they are three, then he is gladdened yet burdened, and if they are four or five, then O servants of Allah, reach him, advance him loans, and enter partnership with him.""**"
"13497","Abdullah ibn Masud (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ speak concerning whoever has a daughter and disciplines her well, teaches her well, and bestows upon her amply from the blessings Allah has bestowed upon him, and what she becomes for him against the Fire. > **""Whoever has a daughter and disciplines her, disciplines her well, teaches her, teaches her well, and bestows upon her amply from the blessings of Allah which He has bestowed upon him, she shall be a protection and a veil for him from the Fire.""**"
"13498","Abu Hurayrah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever has three daughters whom he maintains, shelters, and suffices, and what becomes binding for him. When those present asked about two and then about one, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed the same in each case. > **""Whoever has three daughters whom he maintains, shelters, and suffices, Paradise becomes binding for him, and likewise two, and likewise one.""**"
"13499","Anas (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that a woman with her two daughters visited Aishah (radiya Allahu anha), who gave her three dates. She gave each girl one date, then lifted the third to place it in her own mouth, but when the two girls looked toward her she split it in halves and gave each one a half, then departed. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived, Aishah informed him of what the woman had done, and he announced her entry into Paradise on account of it. > **""She has entered Paradise on account of that.""**"
"13500","Hasan ibn Ali (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that a woman with her two sons came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked of him, so he gave her three dates, one for each of them. She gave each boy his date, then when both looked toward their mother she split the remaining date in halves and gave each a half. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then announced that Allah had shown her mercy on account of her mercy to her two sons. > **""Allah has shown mercy to her on account of her mercy to her two sons.""**"
"13501","Abdullah ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that Abbas (radiya Allahu anhu) would take his son Qutham, place him upon his chest, and chant his love for him, his resemblance to the one of the noble nose, the Prophet of blessings, despite whoever claimed otherwise, within a longer narration of Anas concerning al-Hajjaj ibn Ilat. > **""My beloved Qutham, resembling the one of the noble nose, the Prophet of blessings, despite the dislike of whoever claims otherwise.""**"
"13502","Sahl ibn Sad (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by children playing with dust, and when some of the companions of the Prophet ﷺ forbade them, he ordered that they be left, describing dust in relation to children. > **""Leave them, for dust is the springtime of children.""**"
"13503","Abdullah ibn Umar (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning what a father bestows upon his child and that nothing surpasses good upbringing. > **""No father bestows upon his child anything better than good upbringing.""**"
"13504","Abu Jubayrah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the child across three periods of seven years as a master, then a servant, then a minister, and instructed that if his conduct at twenty-one is pleasing it is well, otherwise to strike his side, for then excuse has been offered before Allah. > **""The child is a master for seven years, a servant for seven years, and a minister for seven years. If you are pleased with his fellowship at twenty-one, then well, otherwise strike his side, for you have offered your excuse to Allah.""**"
"13505","Sakhr ibn Qudamah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning a child born after one hundred years and Allah's need of him, understood to mean that one who fathers a child after completing a hundred years will generally not live to discipline him, so the child learns disobedience. > **""No child born after one hundred years is one in whom Allah has any need.""**"
"13506","Aishah (radiya Allahu anha) reports that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning whoever raises a young child until he says that none is worthy of worship but Allah, and that Allah shall not call him to account. > **""Whoever raises a young child until he says: none is worthy of worship but Allah, Allah shall not call him to account.""**"
"13507","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that a man complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about poor livelihood, so the Prophet ﷺ told him to raise a young one. When the man asked what was meant, the Prophet ﷺ specified a foal, a girl, or a boy. > **""Raise a young one: a foal, a girl, or a boy.""**"
"13508","Abu Hurayrah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that a man complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about hardness of his heart, so the Prophet ﷺ told him to stroke the head of the orphan and feed the destitute. > **""Stroke the head of the orphan and feed the destitute.""**"
"13509","Abu al-Darda (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ complaining of hardness of his heart, and the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he would love his heart to soften and his need to be fulfilled, then enjoined mercy to the orphan, stroking his head, and feeding him from his food, promising a softened heart and a fulfilled need. > **""Would you love that your heart soften and your need be fulfilled? Show mercy to the orphan, stroke his head, and feed him from your food; your heart will soften and you will attain your need.""**"
"13510","Abdullah ibn Umar (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Prophet ﷺ visited a woman of Khatham and asked how she found herself. When she replied that she deemed herself near death on account of her ailment, the Prophet ﷺ expressed his wish that she not leave this world until she had sponsored an orphan or equipped a warrior. > **""How do you find yourself? I would love that you not depart this world until you sponsor an orphan or equip a warrior.""**"
"13511","Aishah (radiya Allahu anha) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened himself and the sponsor of the orphan in Paradise to two fingers, joining his index and middle fingers, and likened one who strives for the orphan, the widow, and the destitute to a warrior in the path of Allah and to one fasting and standing in prayer without pause. > **""I and the sponsor of the orphan shall be in Paradise like these two, and one who strives for the orphan, the widow, and the destitute is like a warrior in the path of Allah and like one fasting and standing who never pauses.""**"
"13512","Abu Musa al-Ashari (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning an orphan who sits with people at their bowl and eats with them, and how Satan keeps away from their bowl on that account. > **""No orphan sits with people at their bowl except that Satan keeps away from their bowl.""**"
"13513","Abdullah ibn Umar (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the most beloved house to Allah as one in which an orphan is honoured. > **""Indeed the most beloved house to Allah is a house in which an orphan is honoured.""**"
"13514","Abu Umamah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever strokes the head of an orphan seeking only Allah thereby, and the good deeds recorded for every hair his hand passes over, and concerning whoever treats well an orphan girl or boy in his care, and his closeness to him in Paradise like two fingers, separating his index and middle fingers in illustration. > **""Whoever strokes the head of an orphan, stroking it only for Allah, shall have good deeds for every hair his hand passes over, and whoever treats well an orphan girl or boy in his care, he and I shall be in Paradise like these two.""**"
"13515","Amr ibn Malik al-Qushayri (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning whoever joins an orphan of two Muslim parents to his food and drink until Allah enriches him, and what becomes binding for him. > **""Whoever joins an orphan of two Muslim parents to his food and drink until Allah enriches him, Paradise becomes binding for him.""**"
"13516","Zurarah ibn Awfa reports from a man of his people called Abu Malik, or Ibn Malik, who heard the Prophet ﷺ speak concerning whoever joins an orphan among Muslims to his food and drink until he becomes independent of him, concerning whoever reaches his parents or one of them and fails to honour them and then enters the Fire far removed by Allah, and concerning any Muslim who frees a Muslim slave and what it becomes as ransom for him from the Fire. > **""Whoever joins an orphan among Muslims to his food and drink until he becomes independent of him, Paradise becomes binding for him without exception. Whoever reaches his parents or one of them and then fails to honour them and enters the Fire, may Allah remove him far, and any Muslim who frees a Muslim slave, it shall be his ransom from the Fire.""**"
"13517","Bashir ibn Aqrabah al-Juhani (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that he met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Uhud and asked what had become of his father. When the Prophet ﷺ informed him that he had been martyred and invoked mercy upon him, the boy wept, so the Prophet ﷺ drew him close, stroked his head, mounted him with himself, and asked whether he would be pleased with him as a father and Aishah as a mother. > **""Your father has been martyred, may the mercy of Allah be upon him. Would you not be pleased that I be your father and Aishah your mother?""**"
"13518","Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that while they sat with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, a boy came and pleaded, offering his father and mother as ransom, that he was an orphan with an orphan sister and a widowed mother, asking for food from what he had until they were pleased. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ praised his words, sent him to his household for whatever food was found, and Bilal (radiya Allahu anhu) brought twenty-one dates placed in the palm of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ gestured with his palms toward his mouth, and those present perceived him invoking blessing, then allotted seven for the boy, seven for his mother, and seven for his sister, instructing him to sup with one date and breakfast with another. When the boy departed, Muadh ibn Jabal (radiya Allahu anhu) rose to him, placed his hand upon his head, prayed that Allah mend his orphanhood and make him a successor to his father, and when asked, explained that he had acted out of mercy. The Prophet ﷺ then swore by Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad concerning whoever takes charge of an orphan among the Muslims, and the rank, good deed, and expiation recorded for every hair. > **""How excellent is what you have said, O boy. By Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, no one among the Muslims takes charge of an orphan except that Allah records for him a rank for every hair, grants him a good deed for every hair, and expiates from him an evil deed for every hair.""**"
"13519","Abu Hurayrah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described himself as the first to open the gate of Paradise, yet a woman would hasten ahead of him, and when he asked her condition and identity she would reply that she was one who had remained seated over her orphans. > **""I am the first to open the gate of Paradise, except that a woman will hasten ahead of me, so I shall say to her: what is your matter and who are you? And she shall say: I am a woman who remained seated over my orphans.""**"
"13520","Abu Hurayrah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever sponsors an orphan, whether kindred or not, and his closeness to him in Paradise like two fingers, joining his fingers in illustration. > **""Whoever sponsors an orphan, whether kindred or not, he and I shall be in Paradise like these two.""**"
"13521","Adi ibn Hatim (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever joins to himself an orphan of his own or of another until Allah enriches him, and what becomes binding for him. > **""Whoever joins to himself an orphan of his own or of another until Allah enriches him independently, Paradise becomes binding for him.""**"
"13522","Abdullah ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever shelters one or two orphans and then bears with patience seeking reward, and his closeness to him in Paradise like two fingers, turning his index and middle fingers in illustration. > **""Whoever shelters an orphan or two orphans, then bears with patience and seeks reward, he and I shall be in Paradise like these two.""**"
"13523","Abu Umamah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened himself and the sponsor of the orphan in Paradise to two fingers. > **""I and the sponsor of the orphan shall be in Paradise like these two.""**"
"13524","Abdullah ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever sponsors an orphan of his own or of another, and what becomes binding for him, except if he has done a deed that is not forgiven. > **""Whoever sponsors an orphan of his own or of another, Paradise becomes binding for him, unless he has done a deed that is not forgiven.""**"
"13525","Abdullah ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Prophet ﷺ spoke concerning any Muslim who takes an orphan among Muslims to his food and drink and his certain entry into Paradise short of an unforgivable sin, concerning one deprived of his two beloved things who bears with patience seeking reward and whose recompense is none but Paradise, explaining the two as his two eyes when asked, and concerning one who maintains three daughters, teaches and marries them, and disciplines them well. When a bedouin asked about two, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed the same. Ibn Abbas remarked that this belongs among the choicest and most precious narrations. > **""There is no Muslim who takes an orphan among Muslims to his food and drink except that he shall enter Paradise without exception, unless he commits a sin that is not forgiven. Whoever is deprived of his two beloved ones and bears with patience seeking reward shall have no reward but Paradise, meaning his two eyes. Whoever maintains three daughters, teaches them, marries them, and disciplines them well, Allah shall admit him into Paradise, and likewise two.""**"
"13526","The daughter of Murrah reports from her father that the Prophet ﷺ spoke concerning the sponsor of an orphan of his own or of another, when joined with piety, and his closeness to him in Paradise like two fingers, meaning the index and the middle, with a further chain through Umm Said, daughter of Murrah al-Fihri, from her father to the same effect. > **""The sponsor of an orphan of his own or of another, when he is God-fearing, shall be with me in Paradise like these two.""**"
"13527","Umm Said bint Amr ibn Murrah al-Jumahiyyah (radiya Allahu anha) reports that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning whoever sponsors an orphan of his own or of any other people, and his closeness to him in Paradise like two fingers. > **""Whoever sponsors an orphan of his own or of others among people, he and I shall be in Paradise like these two.""**"
"13528","Jabir ibn Abdullah (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on what account he might strike his orphan ward, and the Prophet ﷺ permitted it on the same account for which he would strike his own child, provided he did not augment his own wealth through the orphan's wealth nor accumulate wealth from it. > **""Strike him for whatever you would strike your own child, without augmenting your wealth through his wealth nor accumulating wealth from his wealth.""**"
"13529","Abdur Rahman ibn Abza (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined being toward the orphan as a merciful father, in a fuller narration found in the book of renunciation. > **""Be to the orphan like a merciful father.""**"
"13530","Abdullah ibn Abdullah reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described how the head of the orphan is to be stroked, and Salih demonstrated it by placing his palm upon the front of the head near the forehead, carrying it to the middle, then returning it to the front or the forehead, with a like manner for one who has a father. In another wording the Prophet ﷺ distinguished stroking forward for the orphaned boy and from front to back for one with a father. > **""The orphan is to have his head stroked thus, forward, and one who has a father is to have his head stroked thus, from the front backward.""**"
"13531","Abdullah ibn Abbas (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning three claims of the slave upon his master: not to be hurried away from his prayer, not to be made to rise from his food, and to be fed to full satiety. > **""The slave has three claims upon his master: he is not hurried away from his prayer, he is not made to rise from his food, and he is fed to full satiety.""**"
"13532","Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counted among a man's felicity a righteous neighbour, a comfortable mount, and a spacious dwelling. > **""Among the felicity of a man are a righteous neighbour, a comfortable mount, and a spacious dwelling.""**"
"13533","Abdullah ibn Umar (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning Allah warding off tribulation from a hundred households among his neighbours on account of one righteous Muslim, then reciting that were Allah not to repel people by means of one another the earth would be corrupted. > **""Allah wards off tribulation from a hundred households among his neighbours on account of one righteous Muslim.""**"
"13534","Rafi ibn Khadij (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined seeking the neighbour before the dwelling and the companion before the road. > **""Seek the neighbour before the house, and the companion before the road.""**"
"13535","Abu Umamah (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoin the neighbour so insistently that he supposed he would grant him a share of inheritance. > **""He kept enjoining the neighbour until I supposed he would grant him a share of inheritance.""**"
"13536","Jabir ibn Abdullah (radiya Allahu anhuma) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divided neighbours into three: one with a single right and the least among them, one with two rights, and one with three rights. The first is an idolatrous neighbour without kinship, due the right of neighbourhood; the second is a Muslim neighbour, due the right of Islam and the right of neighbourhood; and the third is a Muslim neighbour with kinship, due the right of Islam, the right of neighbourhood, and the right of kinship. > **""Neighbours are three: a neighbour with one right, a neighbour with two rights, and a neighbour with three rights. The Muslim neighbour with kinship is due the right of Islam, the right of neighbourhood, and the right of kinship.""**"
"13537","Said ibn Zayd (radiya Allahu anhu) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ affirmed that the neighbour is due a right. > **""The neighbour is due a right.""**"
"13538","A man of the Ansar reports that he set out with his family intending the Prophet ﷺ and found him standing with a man approaching him, so he sat aside supposing some need between them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained standing so long that pity for him overcame the witness on account of the lengthy standing. When the man departed, the witness rose to him and remarked upon the other's keeping him standing, and the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he knew who he was. Upon his denial, the Prophet ﷺ identified him as Jibril, peace be upon him, who kept enjoining the neighbour until he supposed he would grant him inheritance, adding that had the witness greeted him he would have returned the greeting. > **""Do you know who this was? It was Jibril. He kept enjoining the neighbour until I supposed he would grant him a share of inheritance. Had you greeted him, he would have returned your greeting.""**"
"13539","Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he was passing by when he found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon al-Safa with his cheek placed close against a man, in intimate conversation. He had scarcely waited before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called out to him, asking why he had not given greeting. Muhammad explained that he had seen the Prophet ﷺ engaged with that man in a manner he had shown to no one else, and he had disliked interrupting his conversation, then asked who the man was. The Prophet ﷺ informed him that it was Jibril, peace be upon him, and when asked what he had said, explained that he had kept enjoining the neighbour upon him until he began to expect that he would command him to grant the neighbour a share of inheritance. > **""O Muhammad ibn Maslamah, what kept you from giving greeting? It was Jibril. He kept enjoining the neighbour upon me until I expected he would command me to give him inheritance.""**"
"13540","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man came while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Jibril were praying at the place where the funeral prayer is performed. The man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who the man was whom he had seen with him. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether he had indeed seen him, and when he confirmed it, told him that he had seen an abundant good, for it was Jibril, who had kept enjoining the neighbour upon him until he thought that he would cause the neighbour to inherit. > **""You have seen abundant good. This was Jibril. He kept enjoining the neighbour upon me until I thought he would cause him to inherit.""**"
"13541","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that Jibril kept enjoining the neighbour upon him until he thought that he would cause the neighbour to inherit. > **""Jibril kept enjoining the neighbour upon me until I thought he would cause him to inherit.""**"
"13542","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Jibril kept enjoining the neighbour upon him until he thought that he would cause the neighbour to inherit. > **""Jibril kept enjoining the neighbour upon me until I thought he would cause him to inherit.""**"
"13543","Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Jibril, peace be upon him, had enjoined the neighbour upon him so emphatically that he thought he would surely cause the neighbour to inherit. > **""Jibril enjoined the neighbour upon me until I thought he would surely cause him to inherit.""**"
"13544","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon his camel al-Jad'a during the Farewell Pilgrimage repeatedly enjoining the neighbour, saying that he enjoined the neighbour upon them, and he repeated it so often that the narrator thought he would grant the neighbour inheritance. > **""I enjoin the neighbour upon you.""**"
"13545","Mu'awiyah ibn Haydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what right his neighbour held over him. The Prophet ﷺ instructed him to visit the neighbour when ill, to accompany his funeral when he died, to lend to him when he sought a loan, to conceal his fault when he fell into need, to congratulate him when good befell him, to console him when calamity struck him, not to raise his building above his in a way that blocked the wind from him, and not to harm him with the aroma of his cooking pot unless he ladled out a portion for him from it. > **""If he falls ill, visit him; if he dies, accompany his funeral; if he seeks a loan, lend to him; if he is destitute, conceal his fault; if good befalls him, congratulate him; if calamity befalls him, console him; do not raise your building above his so as to block the wind from him; and do not harm him with the aroma of your pot unless you ladle out some of it for him.""**"
"13546","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when one of them cooked a pot, he should increase its broth and then pass a portion of it to his neighbour. > **""When one of you cooks a pot, let him increase its broth and then give some of it to his neighbour.""**"
"13547","Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that she was once upon land which the Prophet ﷺ had granted to Abu Salamah and al-Zubayr from the land of Banu al-Nadir. Al-Zubayr had gone out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and they had a Jewish neighbour who slaughtered a sheep and cooked it. She caught its aroma, and the craving stirred by the smell of meat seized her as nothing else ever had, while she was pregnant with her daughter called Khadijah. Unable to restrain herself, she went to the man's wife to borrow fire, hoping thereby to be given food, though she had no need of fire. When she smelled it again and saw the meat, her craving intensified. She extinguished the fire she had taken, then returned a second time for the same purpose, then a third. When she saw her state, she sat down weeping and supplicating Allah. The Jewish woman's husband then came and asked whether anyone had visited, and his wife replied that only the Arab woman had come seeking fire. He vowed he would never eat of it unless she sent some of it to her, so she sent a ladleful, a bowlful, to Asma, and at that moment no food on earth was more beloved to her than that meal. > **""I shall never eat of it unless you send some of it to her.""**"
"13548","A'ishah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about her two neighbours, one whose door faced her own door and another whose door lay farther from hers though his wall was nearer, asking with which she should begin. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed her to begin with the one whose door faced her own door. > **""Begin with the one whose door faces your door.""**"
"13549","Mu'awiyah ibn Haydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to which of his two neighbours he should present a gift. The Prophet ﷺ instructed him to give to the nearer of the two by way of his door. > **""Give to the nearer of the two by his door.""**"
"13550","A man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ reported from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should be mindful of Allah and honour his neighbour, whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should be mindful of Allah and honour his guest, and whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should speak what is true or remain silent. > **""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him fear Allah and honour his neighbour; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him fear Allah and honour his guest; and whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak truth or remain silent.""**"
"13551","In another narration, reported three times over, whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should honour his guest, and, again repeated three times, whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should treat his neighbour well. > **""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honour his guest. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him treat his neighbour well.""**"
"13552","A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should not harm his neighbour, whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should speak good or keep silent, and whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should honour his guest. > **""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not harm his neighbour; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak good or keep silent; and whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honour his guest.""**"
"13553","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should honour his guest, whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should safeguard his neighbour, and whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should speak good or remain silent. > **""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honour his guest; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him safeguard his neighbour; and whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent.""**"
"13554","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that one who spent the night satiated while his neighbour beside him went hungry, knowing of it, had not truly believed in him. > **""He has not believed in me who spends the night full while his neighbour beside him is hungry and he knows of it.""**"
"13555","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported while in the palm grove of Ibn al-Zubayr that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the one who ate his fill while his neighbour went hungry was not a true believer. > **""He is not a believer who eats his fill while his neighbour is hungry.""**"
"13556","Abayah ibn Rifa'ah reported that it reached Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that Sa'd, after building the palace, had remarked that the faint sound had been cut off. Umar sent Muhammad ibn Maslamah to him. When he arrived, he produced his fire-drill, kindled his fire, and purchased firewood for a dirham. Sa'd was told that a man had done such and such, and he recognized him as Muhammad ibn Maslamah. He came out to him and swore by Allah that he had not said the remark. Muhammad replied that he would convey on his behalf what he stated and carry out what he had been commanded to do, then turned to the door and set it alight, declined an invitation to be hosted, and set out back to Umar, hastening to him, his going and return taking nineteen nights. Umar remarked that but for his good opinion of him he would have thought he had not discharged the mission. He answered that Sa'd sent greeting, offered excuse, and swore by Allah he had not uttered the words. Umar asked whether Sa'd had provisioned him, and he said no. Umar asked what had kept him from provisioning himself on his behalf, and he replied that he had disliked commanding provisions for him such that the cool comfort would be his while the burden of heat fell upon himself, when around him were the people of Madinah whom hunger had struck, adding that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that a man should not eat his fill to the exclusion of his neighbour. > **""A man does not eat his fill to the exclusion of his neighbour.""**"
"13557","Ali, may Allah honour his face, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that he would not give to them while neglecting the People of Suffah whose bellies were curled with hunger. > **""I shall not give to you while neglecting the People of Suffah with their bellies curled up from hunger.""**"
"13558","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ asking for clothing. The Prophet ﷺ turned away from him, the man repeated his plea for clothing, and the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he had no neighbour possessing two spare garments. When the man replied that he had, more than one, the Prophet ﷺ declared that Allah would not gather him and such a neighbour together in Paradise. > **""Have you no neighbour with two spare garments? Allah will not gather you and him together in Paradise.""**"
"13559","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the right of neighbourhood extended to forty houses, thus and thus and thus and thus, to the right and to the left, before and behind, as indicated by gesture. > **""The right of the neighbour extends to forty houses: thus and thus and thus and thus, to the right and to the left, before and behind.""**"
"13560","Fadalah ibn Ubayd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that three were among the ruinous afflictions: a ruler who, if one did good, showed no gratitude, and if one did ill, did not forgive; an evil neighbour who, if he saw good, buried it, and if he saw evil, broadcast it; and a wife who harmed her husband when present and betrayed him when he was absent from her. > **""Three are among the ruinous: a ruler who shows no thanks if you do good and grants no pardon if you do ill; an evil neighbour who buries good when he sees it and broadcasts evil when he sees it; and a wife who harms you when present and betrays you when absent.""**"
"13561","Al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked his Companions what they said concerning adultery. They replied that it was forbidden, made unlawful by Allah and His Messenger, unlawful until the Day of Resurrection. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then told them that for a man to commit adultery with ten women was lighter upon him than to commit adultery with the wife of his neighbour. He then asked what they said concerning theft. They replied that Allah and His Messenger had forbidden it, so it was unlawful. He declared that for a man to steal from ten houses was lighter upon him than to steal from his neighbour. > **""For a man to commit adultery with ten women is lighter upon him than to commit adultery with the wife of his neighbour. For a man to steal from ten houses is lighter upon him than to steal from his neighbour.""**"
"13562","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man mentioned a certain woman, describing her abundant prayer, charity, and fasting, except that she harmed her neighbours with her tongue. The Prophet ﷺ said she was in the Fire. The man then mentioned another woman, describing her scant fasting and prayer, noting that she gave pieces of dried curd in charity and did not harm her neighbours with her tongue. The Prophet ﷺ said she was in Paradise. > **""She is in the Fire. She is in Paradise.""**"
"13563","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swore thrice by Allah that such a one did not believe. When asked whom he meant, he said it was the neighbour from whose injurious conduct his neighbour was not safe. When asked what his injurious conduct meant, he said it was his evil. > **""By Allah, he does not believe; by Allah, he does not believe; by Allah, he does not believe: the neighbour from whose injurious conduct his neighbour is not safe. His injurious conduct is his evil.""**"
"13564","Talq ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that he was not a believer whose neighbour was not safe from his injurious conduct. > **""He is not a believer whose neighbour is not safe from his injurious conduct.""**"
"13565","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that he was not a believer whose neighbour was not safe from his injurious conduct. > **""He is no believer whose neighbour is not safe from his injurious conduct.""**"
"13566","Ka'b ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ saying that he had settled in the quarter of a certain tribe and that the harshest harm came to him from the nearest in neighbourhood. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ therefore sent Abu Bakr, Umar, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, to stand at the door of the mosque and proclaim that forty houses constituted neighbourhood, and that one whose neighbour feared his injurious conduct would not enter Paradise. > **""Know that forty houses are neighbours, and one whose neighbour fears his injurious conduct shall not enter Paradise.""**"
"13567","Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man came to Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, asking whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had left with her anything she treasured as a keepsake. She called for her slave girl to bring the folded document, which the girl sought but could not find, whereupon Fatimah told her to seek it diligently, for in her estimation it equalled Hasan and Husayn. The girl searched and found that she had swept it up among the sweepings. Within it was recorded that Muhammad ﷺ had said that among the believers was not one from whose injurious conduct his neighbour was not safe, that whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should honour his guest, that whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should not harm his neighbour, that whoever believed in Allah and the Last Day should speak good or remain silent, that Allah loved the modest, forbearing, and chaste who refrained from begging, and hated the obscene, foul-tongued beggar who importuned, and that modesty was of faith and faith led to Paradise, while obscenity sprang from foulness and foulness led to the Fire. > **""He is not among the believers whose neighbour is not safe from his injurious conduct. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honour his guest. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not harm his neighbour. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent. Allah loves the modest, forbearing, chaste one, and hates the obscene, foul-tongued, importunate beggar. Modesty is of faith, and faith is in Paradise; obscenity is of foulness, and foulness is in the Fire.""**"
"13568","Abu Juhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ complaining of his neighbour. The Prophet ﷺ told him to lay his goods upon the road. He did so, and people passing by began to curse the offender. The offending neighbour then came to the Prophet ﷺ asking what he had suffered from people, and when questioned what he had met with, complained that they cursed him. The Prophet ﷺ replied that Allah had cursed him before the people did. The man vowed never to repeat his harm, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ told the complainant to lift his goods, for he had been sufficed against the harm. > **""Lay your goods upon the road. Allah cursed you before the people did. Lift your goods, for you have been sufficed.""**"
"13569","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no harm done to the neighbour was to be deemed trifling. > **""No harm of the neighbour is trifling.""**"
"13570","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever peered from the house of his neighbour and looked upon the private matters of his Muslim brother, or upon the hair of his wife, or upon anything of her body, it became due upon Allah to admit him into the Fire. > **""Whoever peers from his neighbour's house and looks upon the private matters of his Muslim brother, or the hair of his wife, or anything of her body, it is due upon Allah to admit him into the Fire.""**"
"13571","Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out for a military expedition and declared that none who had harmed his neighbour should accompany them that day. A man among the people confessed that he had urinated at the base of his neighbour's wall, and the Prophet ﷺ told him not to accompany them that day. > **""None who has harmed his neighbour shall accompany us today. Do not accompany us today.""**"
"13572","Uqbah ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the first two litigants on the Day of Resurrection would be two neighbours. > **""The first two litigants on the Day of Resurrection shall be two neighbours.""**"
"13573","Mutarrif ibn Abdullah reported that accounts of a narration from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, reached him, and he longed to meet him. When he met him he addressed him as Abu Dharr, explained that the narration had reached him, and asked him to relate it concerning Allah loving three and hating three. Abu Dharr replied that he did not consider himself one who would lie against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When asked who the three whom Allah loved were, he described first a man who campaigned in the path of Allah with patience and hope of reward, fighting until slain, whom they would find mentioned in the Book of Allah, then recited that Allah loved those who fought in His cause in ranks as though a compact structure. When asked who else, he described a man with an evil neighbour who harmed him, yet who bore the harm patiently until Allah sufficed him against that neighbour through life or death. > **""Allah loves three: a man who campaigns in the path of Allah, patient and hopeful of reward, and fights until he is slain; and a man with an evil neighbour who harms him, yet he bears his harm patiently until Allah suffices him against him through life or death.""**"
"13574","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet ﷺ established brotherhood between al-Zubayr and Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both. > **""The Prophet established brotherhood between al-Zubayr and Ibn Mas'ud.""**"
"13575","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established brotherhood among his Companions, joining Salman and Abu al-Darda, and joining Awf ibn Malik and Sa'b ibn Jaththamah, may Allah be pleased with them. > **""The Messenger of Allah established brotherhood among his Companions: between Salman and Abu al-Darda, and between Awf ibn Malik and Sa'b ibn Jaththamah.""**"
"13576","Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he had joined him and Hamzah together in brotherhood. > **""O Messenger of Allah, you have joined me and Hamzah in brotherhood.""**"
"13577","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established brotherhood between Zayd ibn Harithah and Hamzah, may Allah be pleased with them both. > **""The Messenger of Allah established brotherhood between Zayd ibn Harithah and Hamzah.""**"
"13578","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that Zayd ibn Harithah, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, became the brother of Hamzah, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had established brotherhood between them. > **""Zayd ibn Harithah, freedman of the Messenger of Allah, became the brother of Hamzah; the Messenger of Allah had joined them in brotherhood.""**"
"13579","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported in another narration that Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, said concerning the daughter of Hamzah that she was the daughter of his brother, since the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had established brotherhood between him and her father. > **""She is the daughter of my brother; the Messenger of Allah joined me and her father in brotherhood.""**"
"13580","Amr ibn Qays and Asal ibn Ka'b, of Banu Zimam, reported from his grandfather Mazin ibn Khaythamah that Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, sent them forth when he had alighted between al-Sakun and al-Sakasik, until the people embraced Islam and came in delegations to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who established brotherhood between al-Sakun and al-Sakasik. > **""The Messenger of Allah established brotherhood between al-Sakun and al-Sakasik.""**"
"13581","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established brotherhood between Abu al-Darda and Salman, may Allah be pleased with them both. > **""The Messenger of Allah established brotherhood between Abu al-Darda and Salman.""**"
"13582","Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he had witnessed the alliance of al-Mutayyabin alongside his uncles while yet a youth, and that he would not wish to break it even for red camels. Al-Zuhri added that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Islam encountered no alliance but that it strengthened it further, and that there was no formal alliance in Islam, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ himself fostered affection between Quraysh and the Ansar. > **""I witnessed the alliance of al-Mutayyabin with my uncles when I was a youth; I would not love to break it even for red camels. Islam meets no alliance but that it strengthens it; and there is no alliance in Islam.""**"
"13583","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that it would not please him to possess red camels in exchange for breaking the alliance concluded in Dar al-Nadwah. > **""It would not please me to have red camels and to have broken the alliance concluded in Dar al-Nadwah.""**"
"13584","Budayl ibn Warqa reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ included Khuza'ah in the alliance on the day of Hudaybiyah, and wrote to them, and to Budayl ibn Warqa and the chiefs of Banu Amr, conveying greeting, praising Allah beside Whom there is no deity, then stating that he had not deemed their affair sinful nor set anything against them, that the dearest of Tihamah to him were they and those of the allies who followed them, that he had secured for the emigrant among them what he had secured for himself, even if one emigrated in his own land without residing in Makkah, and that they need neither fear from his side nor cause fear, or words to that effect. > **""Peace be upon you; I praise Allah, beside Whom there is no deity, unto you. I have not deemed your affair sinful nor set anything against you. The dearest of Tihamah to me are you and those allies who follow you. I have secured for your emigrant what I secured for myself. You are neither to fear from my side nor to be made afraid.""**"
"13585","Salamah ibn Budayl ibn Warqa reported that his father Budayl ibn Warqa handed him this letter, telling him that it was the letter of the Prophet ﷺ, that they should treasure its counsel, and that they would remain in good so long as it endured among them. The letter opened in the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, from Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, to Budayl ibn Warqa and Bishr and the chiefs of Banu Amr, praising Allah beside Whom there is no deity, declaring that he had not deemed their affair sinful nor set anything against them, that they were the dearest of Tihamah to him and nearest in kinship, with those allies who followed them, that he had secured for their emigrant what he had secured for himself, even if he emigrated in his own land without residing in Makkah except as a pilgrim performing lesser or greater pilgrimage, that he had not neglected them nor surrendered them, that they were neither to fear from his side nor to be besieged, and further announcing that Alqamah ibn Ulathah and the two sons of Awf had embraced Islam and pledged allegiance on behalf of those of Ikrimah who followed them, securing for his followers among them what he secured for himself, and that they were of one another forever, in inviolable and violable territory. Abu Muhammad added that his father heard their elders say that it was in the handwriting of Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him. > **""In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Budayl ibn Warqa and the chiefs of Banu Amr: I praise Allah, beside Whom there is no deity, unto you. I have not deemed your affair sinful nor set anything against you. You are the dearest of Tihamah to me and nearest in kinship. I have secured for your emigrant what I secured for myself. You are neither to fear from my side nor to be besieged. We are of one another forever, in inviolable and violable territory.""**"
"13586","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that there was no formal alliance in Islam, and that whatever alliance had existed in the pre-Islamic age, Islam only increased it in strength and firmness. > **""There is no alliance in Islam; whatever alliance existed in the pre-Islamic age, Islam only increased it in strength.""**"
"13587","Qays ibn Asim, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he asked the Prophet ﷺ concerning alliance, and the Prophet ﷺ replied that whatever alliance had existed in the pre-Islamic age they should hold fast to, but that there was no new alliance in Islam. > **""Whatever alliance existed in the pre-Islamic age, hold fast to it; but there is no alliance in Islam.""**"
"13588","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that there was no formal alliance in Islam, and that whatever alliance had existed in the pre-Islamic age, Islam only increased it in strength. > **""There is no alliance in Islam; whatever alliance existed in the pre-Islamic age, Islam only increased it in strength.""**"
"13589","Furat ibn Hibban al-Ijli, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning the pre-Islamic alliance. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether he was perhaps inquiring about the alliance of Lakhm and Tamim. When he confirmed it, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied that Islam only increased it in strength. > **""Are you asking about the alliance of Lakhm and Tamim? Islam only increases it in strength.""**"
"13590","Abdullah ibn Qays, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ frequently visited the Ansar, both in particular and in general. When he visited in particular, he would come to the man in his house, and when he visited in general, he would come to the mosque. > **""The Messenger of Allah often visited the Ansar, in particular and in general: in particular to the man in his house, and in general at the mosque.""**"
"13591","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that no Muslim servant who visited his brother for the sake of Allah was left without a caller from heaven proclaiming that he was blessed and Paradise was made pleasant for him. Rather Allah declared in the dominion of His Throne that His servant had visited Him for His sake and that his entertainment lay upon Him, and He was not content with any reward for him short of Paradise. > **""No Muslim visits his brother for the sake of Allah but that a caller from heaven proclaims: you are blessed and Paradise is made pleasant for you. My servant has visited Me for My sake, and his entertainment is upon Me; I am not content with any reward for him short of Paradise.""**"
"13592","Abu Razin al-Uqayli, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when a Muslim visited his Muslim brother, seventy thousand angels accompanied him, invoking blessings upon him and praying that Allah join him even as he had joined his brother for His sake. > **""O Abu Razin, when a Muslim visits his Muslim brother, seventy thousand angels accompany him, invoking blessings upon him, saying: O Allah, even as he has joined him for Your sake, so join him.""**"
"13593","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he should inform them of their men destined for Paradise. When they replied affirmatively, he said that the Prophet was in Paradise, the truthful defender of faith was in Paradise, and the man who visited his brother in a distant quarter of the town, visiting him for none but Allah, was in Paradise. > **""Shall I not inform you of your men in Paradise? The Prophet is in Paradise, the truthful one is in Paradise, and the man who visits his brother in a distant quarter of the town, visiting him for none but Allah, is in Paradise.""**"
"13594","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to her to prepare the sitting place for them, for an angel was descending to the earth who had never descended to it before. > **""Prepare the sitting place for us, for an angel is descending to the earth who has never descended to it before.""**"
"13595","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to join two of his Companions together in brotherhood, and the night would grow long upon one of them until he met his brother, greeting him with affection and kindness and asking how he had fared after him. As for people in general now, three days might pass over one of them without knowing the condition of his brother. > **""How have you been after me?""**"
"13596","Umm Nujayd, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to visit them among Banu Amr ibn Awf, and she would prepare barley drink in a large cup, giving him to drink from it when he came. > **""The Messenger of Allah would visit us among Banu Amr ibn Awf, and I would prepare barley drink for him in a cup and give him to drink when he came.""**"
"13597","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that he entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who tossed toward him a cushion stuffed with palm fibre. He did not sit upon it, and it remained between him and the Prophet ﷺ. > **""He tossed me a cushion stuffed with palm fibre, and I did not sit upon it; it remained between me and him.""**"
"13598","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that Umar visited Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with them both, who tossed a cushion to him. Umar asked what this was, and Salman replied that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that no Muslim visited by his Muslim brother, who then offered him a cushion out of honour and esteem, was left without Allah forgiving him. > **""No Muslim is visited by his Muslim brother, who offers him a cushion out of honour and esteem, but that Allah forgives him.""**"
"13599","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that Salman visited Umar while the latter was reclining upon a cushion. Umar tossed it toward him, and Salman said that there was no Muslim who visited his Muslim brother and was offered a cushion out of honour but that Allah forgave him. > **""O Salman, no Muslim visits his Muslim brother, who offers him a cushion out of honour, but that Allah forgives him.""**"
"13600","Jubayr ibn Mut'im, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to go with him to Banu Waqif to visit al-Basir, a man whose sight had been taken away. > **""Come with us to Banu Waqif to visit al-Basir.""**"
"13601","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to go with him to Banu Waqif to visit al-Basir. > **""Come with us to Banu Waqif to visit al-Basir.""**"
"13602","Awf reported that Abdullah, meaning Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, asked his companions when they came to him whether they sat together. They replied that they did not abandon that. He asked whether they visited one another, and they replied affirmatively, explaining that a man among them would miss his brother and walk on foot to the farthest end of Kufah until he met him. Abdullah told them they would remain in good so long as they did so. > **""Do you sit together? Do you visit one another? So long as you do so, you shall remain in good.""**"
"13603","Habib ibn Ibrahim ibn-subayt reported that he entered upon Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz al-Zubaydi, may Allah be pleased with him, who tossed toward him the cushion beneath him, declaring that whoever did not honour his companion belonged neither to Ahmad nor to Ibrahim, peace be upon them both. > **""Whoever does not honour his companion is neither of Ahmad nor of Ibrahim.""**"
"13604","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him to visit at intervals, and love would increase. > **""O Abu Hurayrah, visit at intervals; love will increase.""**"
"13605","Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to visit at intervals, and love would increase. > **""Visit at intervals; love will increase.""**"
"13606","Habib ibn Salamah al-Fihri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to visit at intervals, and love would increase. > **""Visit at intervals; love will increase.""**"
"13607","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to visit at intervals, and love would increase. > **""Visit at intervals; love will increase.""**"
"13608","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to visit at intervals, and love would increase. > **""Visit at intervals; love will increase.""**"
"13609","Uqbah ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that there was no good in one who offered no hospitality to the guest. > **""There is no good in one who shows no hospitality.""**"
"13610","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that whenever a guest alighted among a people and woke deprived, he was entitled to take the measure of his due entertainment without blame upon him. > **""Whenever a guest alights among a people and wakes deprived, he may take the measure of his entertainment, and there is no blame upon him.""**"
"13611","Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command hospitality toward the guest. > **""Entertain the guest.""**"
"13612","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that the guest held a right of three days against the one with whom he alighted, whatever exceeded that being charity, while the guest was obliged to depart without bringing sin upon the household of his host. > **""The guest holds a right of three days against the one with whom he alights; whatever exceeds that is charity. The guest must depart without bringing sin upon the household.""**"
"13613","Al-Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that hospitality for three days was a binding right, and whatever came beyond that was charity. > **""Hospitality for three days is a binding right; whatever is beyond that is charity.""**"
"13614","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ thrice bound faith in Allah and the Last Day to generous treatment of the guest, and when his companions asked him to define that honour he fixed its duration and named what exceeds it as charity. > **""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honour his guest. Honouring the guest is three days, and whatever extends beyond that is charity upon him.""**"
"13615","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, transmits from the Prophet ﷺ a measure for hospitality, a ruling for what surpasses it, and a general principle encompassing every act of kindness. > **""Hospitality is three days, and whatever exceeds it is charity, and every act of kindness is charity.""**"
"13616","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ defined the limit of hospitality and described the provision offered after that limit as charity. > **""Hospitality is three days, and whatever exceeds it is charity.""**"
"13617","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger ﷺ set a three-day limit for hospitality and judged anything beyond it to be charity. > **""Hospitality is three days, and whatever exceeds it is charity.""**"
"13618","Abu Malik al-Ashjai reports from his father Tariq رضي الله عنه that the Prophet ﷺ limited hospitality to three days and characterised anything above that as an act of recognised goodness. > **""Hospitality is three days, and whatever is beyond that is an act of kindness.""**"
"13619","Zayd ibn Khalid رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ joined honour for the guest, restraint of the tongue except for good speech, and the three-day measure of hospitality whose excess is charity, all to faith in Allah and the Last Day. > **""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honour his guest. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent. Hospitality is three days, and whatever exceeds it is charity.""**"
"13620","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger ﷺ made avoidance of harm to the neighbour, honour toward the guest, and speech confined to good or silence into marks of belief in Allah and the Last Day. > **""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not harm his neighbour. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honour his guest. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent.""**"
"13621","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ tied honouring the guest and restricting speech to good or silence to faith in Allah and the Last Day. > **""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honour his guest. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent.""**"
"13622","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger ﷺ made honouring the guest, speaking good or keeping silence, and honouring the neighbour into requirements of faith in Allah and the Last Day. > **""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honour his guest. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honour his neighbour.""**"
"13623","Humayd al-Tawil reports that when visitors came to visit Anas رضي الله عنه during his illness, Anas called for whatever food was present, even mere morsels, and cited what he had heard from the Messenger ﷺ concerning noble character and Paradise. > **""Noble traits of character are among the deeds of Paradise.""**"
"13624","Shihab ibn Abbad reports that he heard members of the delegation of Abd al-Qays describe their arrival to the Messenger ﷺ, the joy of the people at their coming, the welcome and supplication of the Prophet ﷺ, his inquiry after their chief, their indication of al-Mundhir ibn Aidh, his recognition of him as al-Ashajj by the scar from a donkey's hoof, the delay of al-Ashajj to secure the mounts and baggage before changing from travel clothes into fine garments, the Prophet ﷺ drawing in his outstretched leg and seating him at his right with kindness, his detailed knowledge of their villages of al-Safa, al-Mushakkar and other settlements of Hajar, his address to the Ansar to honour their likeness in Islam, the delegation's praise the next morning of soft bedding, wholesome food and instruction in the Book and the Sunnah, the Prophet's ﷺ delight, his distribution of the men among teachers until they learned the salutations, the Opening of the Book, chapters and practices, his request for their provisions, their heaps of dates, his questions distinguishing Tadud, Sarfan and Barni and his commendation of Barni, their subsequent cultivation of it, and the ruling on vessels and drinks given after al-Ashajj complained of their heavy unhealthy land, together with the warning illustrated by al-Harith of Bani Udal whose leg had been struck with a sword in intoxication. > **""Who is your chief and your leader? Is this al-Ashajj? Here, O Ashajj. I have trodden your lands and they were laid open to me. O company of the Ansar, honour your brothers, for they resemble you in Islam in hair and skin, submitting willingly without compulsion or rancour when others refused submission until they were slain. How did you find the honour and hospitality of your brothers toward you? Do you have any of your provisions with you? Do you call this Tadud? Do you call this Sarfan? Do you call this Barni? It is among the best and most beneficial of your dates. Do not drink from the gourd, the varnished jar, or the hollowed stump, but let each of you drink from a waterskin whose mouth is tied. O Ashajj, if I permit you this much, you will drink that much, until when one of you becomes intoxicated by his drink he rises against his cousin and cuts his leg with the sword.""**"
"13625","Numayr ibn Kharashah al-Thaqafi رضي الله عنه reports that his delegation met the Messenger ﷺ at al-Juhfah, that the people rejoiced at their coming, that they embraced Islam, that the Prophet ﷺ ordered them onward to Madinah, and that each evening he urged the people to host them according to the capacity Allah had granted, whereupon men took one, two, and in the case of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه three guests. > **""Your brothers are your guests, so let each man provide according to what Allah has bestowed upon him.""**"
"13626","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ enjoined abundant praise upon the recipient of blessing, seeking forgiveness upon the one burdened with cares, frequent utterance of the phrase of utter dependence upon Allah for delayed provision, abstention from voluntary fasting without hosts' permission for one staying among people, and sitting where householders direct, since they best know the private aspects of their home, with the added description of resentful malice in envy, laziness in worship, and constriction in livelihood as sin incurring displeasure against its bearer. > **""Whoever is clothed by Allah with a blessing, let him increase in praise of Allah. Whoever is weighed down by cares, let him seek forgiveness. Whoever finds his provision slow in reaching him, let him often say there is no power nor strength except through Allah. Whoever stays among people as guest, let him not fast except with their permission. Whoever enters the house of people, let him sit where they instruct him, for the people know best the private places of their house. Among the sin that brings displeasure upon its bearer are malice in envy, laziness in worship, and hardship in livelihood.""**"
"13627","Shaqiq, or another narrator with Qays expressing doubt over the precise link, reports that when a man visited Salman رضي الله عنه, Salman offered what was at hand and explained that but for the prohibition of the Messenger ﷺ against one companion imposing hardship upon another for his sake, he would have gone to elaborate lengths for him. > **""Were it not that the Messenger ﷺ forbade us, or were it not that we were forbidden that one of us burden himself for his companion, I would have burdened myself for you.""**"
"13628","Shaqiq ibn Salamah reports that he and a companion visited Salman al-Farisi رضي الله عنه, that Salman disavowed elaborate preparation because of the prohibition of pretentious burden, brought bread and salt, pawned his ablution vessel at the companion's remark about wild thyme in order to fetch it, and after the meal gently rebuked the companion's claim of contentment by noting the pawned vessel. > **""Were it not that the Messenger ﷺ forbade pretentious burden, I would have burdened myself for you. If you had been content with what was provided for you, my ablution vessel would not be pawned.""**"
"13629","In a version transmitted from the same narrator concerning Salman رضي الله عنه, the Messenger ﷺ is reported to have forbidden his followers to assume hardship for a guest in matters beyond their means. > **""The Messenger ﷺ forbade us to burden ourselves for the guest with what we do not possess.""**"
"13630","Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr reports that Jabir رضي الله عنه entered his house with a group of the companions of the Prophet ﷺ, that bread and vinegar were set before them, that Jabir bade them eat while praising vinegar and citing the Messenger ﷺ on the ruin of despising provision, whether by the host toward what his house contains or by the guests toward what is presented, with the variant wording that mere contempt for what is brought suffices as evil for a man. > **""What an excellent condiment is vinegar. It is ruin for a man that visitors from among his brothers enter upon him and he despises what is in his house to set before them, and ruin for the people that they despise what is set before them. It suffices a man as evil that he despise what is brought near to him.""**"
"13631","Abu Awanah reports that he prepared food and invited Sulayman al-Amash, that word reached him of the guest's disparagement of the meal as a bare bone and sour pomegranate, that he complained to Raqabah ibn Masqalah, and that Raqabah confronted the guest with sharp reproof of his harsh temperament, perpetual gloom, quick boredom, disregard for the right of visitation, and constriction when asked for wisdom. > **""O Abu Muhammad, a brother of ours invited you and honoured you, yet you say it was over a bare bone and sour pomegranate. By Allah, I know you only as harsh in nature, ever frowning, quick to weary, careless of the right of visitation, as though mustard were blown into your nose when wisdom is sought from you.""**"
"13632","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ instructed a man who eats food or drinks a beverage offered by his Muslim brother to partake without questioning its source. > **""When one of you enters upon his Muslim brother and he feeds him food, let him eat from his food and not ask about it. If he gives him drink to drink, let him drink from his drink and not ask about it.""**"
"13633","Al-Ashath ibn Qays رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ identified those most thankful toward people as those most thankful toward Allah the Blessed and Exalted. > **""The most thankful of people to Allah the Blessed and Exalted are the most thankful of them to people.""**"
"13634","In another version of the report of al-Ashath ibn Qays رضي الله عنه, the Messenger ﷺ negated divine gratitude from one lacking gratitude toward people. > **""Whoever does not thank people has not thanked Allah.""**"
"13635","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger ﷺ often asked her about her verses on gratitude, that upon his specification she recited lines on raising one's beneficence so its weakness does not recoil, on requital and praise as equivalent, and on the bond of the noble never proving frayed and weak, and that he then described the questioning on the Day of Gathering of a servant favoured through another servant and the divine rejection of gratitude directed only upward while bypassing the intermediary. > **""O Aishah, what has become of your verses? Concerning gratitude. O Aishah, when Allah gathers creation on the Day of Resurrection, He will say to a servant of His to whom another servant of His extended a favour: Did you thank him? He will say: My Lord, I knew that was from You, so I thanked You for it. Then He will say: You have not thanked Me if you did not thank the one upon whose hands I caused it to flow. Raise your beneficence lest its weakness recoil upon you one day, and consequences overtake it grown full. He will requite you or praise you, and whoever praises you for what you did is as one who requited. When you seek the bond of the noble, you will not find its rope worn and feeble.""**"
"13636","Abu al-Malih reports from Usamah رضي الله عنه, from the Prophet ﷺ, the negation of gratitude to Allah from one who is ungrateful to people. > **""Whoever does not thank people has not thanked Allah.""**"
"13637","Usamah ibn Zayd رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger ﷺ declared the most grateful toward people to be the most grateful toward Allah the Mighty and Majestic. > **""The most grateful of people to Allah the Mighty and Majestic are the most grateful of them to people.""**"
"13638","Jarir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ denied divine thanks to one who withholds thanks from people. > **""Whoever does not give thanks to people has not given thanks to Allah.""**"
"13639","Abu Said رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ denied thanks to Allah the Mighty and Majestic from one unthankful to people. > **""Whoever does not thank people has not thanked Allah the Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"13640","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger ﷺ commanded requital for every favour, continued supplication until gratitude is recognised when requital is beyond ability, and named Allah as Appreciative who loves the grateful, with the variant wording measuring supplication until the benefactor sees himself recompensed. > **""Whoever extends a favour to you, requite him. If you are unable to requite him, supplicate for him until he knows that you have thanked him, for Allah is Appreciative and loves the thankful, until you consider that you have recompensed him.""**"
"13641","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger ﷺ commanded requital for a favour, remembrance of it when requital is impossible as sufficient thanks, and warned that feigning satiety with what one was never given resembles wearing two garments of falsehood. > **""Whoever receives a favour, let him requite it. Whoever cannot, let him mention it, for whoever mentions it has thanked for it. Whoever feigns satiety with what he was not given is like the wearer of two garments of falsehood.""**"
"13642","Talhah, meaning Ibn Ubaydillah رضي الله عنه, reports that the Messenger ﷺ commanded mention of a received favour, equated its mention with thanks, and equated its concealment with ingratitude. > **""Whoever is granted a favour, let him mention it. Whoever mentions it has thanked for it, and whoever conceals it has been ungrateful for it.""**"
"13643","Al-Hakam ibn Umayr رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ commanded requital for a favour and supplication when means for requital are lacking. > **""Whoever brings a favour to you, requite him. If you find no means, then supplicate for him.""**"
"13644","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ regarded the supplication for divine reward with good as the fullest expression of praise. > **""When a man says: May Allah reward you with good, he has excelled in praise.""**"
"13645","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that while the Messenger ﷺ walked in intense heat the strap of his sandal broke, that a man brought a strap and fitted it to his sandal, and that the Messenger ﷺ cautioned him against deeming small the act of having supplied the Messenger ﷺ, if its weight were known. > **""If you knew what you placed upon the Messenger ﷺ, what you placed upon the Messenger ﷺ would not seem small to you.""**"
"13646","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما states his principle of answering a blessing with its like even if uttered by Pharaoh, though Pharaoh has passed away. > **""If Pharaoh were to say to me: May Allah bless you, I would say: And upon you.""**"
"13647","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ placed the completion of a favour above its initiation in merit. > **""The completion of a favour is better than its beginning.""**"
"13648","Al-Numan ibn Bashir رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger ﷺ linked ingratitude for little to ingratitude for much, ingratitude to people to ingratitude to Allah, proclamation of divine blessing to thanks and its abandonment to denial, and unity to mercy with division as punishment. > **""Whoever does not thank for little will not thank for much. Whoever does not thank people will not thank Allah. Speaking of the blessing of Allah is thanks, and abandoning it is denial. Unity is mercy, and division is punishment.""**"
"13649","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that when one beggar disdained the single date ordered for him by the Prophet ﷺ while another received a single date with glorification of Allah at receiving a date from the Messenger ﷺ, the Prophet ﷺ directed his maidservant to fetch the forty dirhams kept with Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها for the grateful man. > **""Glory be to Allah, a date from the Messenger ﷺ. Go to Umm Salamah and give him the forty dirhams that are with her.""**"
"13650","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ would greet a man by asking after him, that the man first answered with praise of Allah and received a prayer for continued well-being, but that when on another day he answered conditionally upon his own thanks, the Prophet ﷺ fell silent, and upon being questioned explained that hesitation had met with silence. > **""O So-and-so, how are you? May Allah keep you well. I used to ask you and you would say: Well, I praise Allah, so I would say: May Allah keep you well. Today you said: Well, if I give thanks, so you doubted, and I remained silent toward you.""**"
"13651","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ stood among seated people, thrice offered to distinguish their best from their worst amid their silence until one man assented, then defined the best as those whose good is hoped for and whose evil is secure, and the worst as the opposite. > **""Shall I inform you of the best of you as distinct from the worst of you? The best of you are those whose good is hoped for and whose evil is safe. The worst of you are those whose good is not hoped for and whose evil is not safe.""**"
"13652","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger ﷺ repeatedly asked permission to disclose successive grades of the worst: those who alight alone, flog their slaves and withhold assistance; those who hate people and are hated; those who overlook no slip, accept no excuse and forgive no sin; and finally those whose good is not hoped for and whose evil is not safe. > **""Shall I not inform you of your worst? Your worst are those who alight alone, flog their slaves, and withhold assistance. Shall I not inform you of worse than that? Those who hate people and whom people hate. Shall I not inform you of worse than that? Those who overlook no slip, accept no excuse, and forgive no sin. Shall I not inform you of worse than that? Those whose good is not hoped for and whose evil is not safe.""**"
"13653","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ offered to name the worst, and upon assent defined the worst as those whose evil is guarded against without hope of good, and the best as those whose good is hoped for without need to guard against evil. > **""Shall I not inform you of your worst? Your worst are those whose evil is feared and whose good is not hoped for. Your best are those whose good is hoped for and whose evil is not feared.""**"
"13654","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ restricted the apt placement of kindness to persons of lineage, religion, or forbearance. > **""Kindness is fitting only toward one of lineage, religion, or forbearance.""**"
"13655","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger ﷺ commanded restriction of one's house to the God-fearing and restriction of one's beneficence to the believer. > **""Admit none into your house except the God-fearing, and bestow your kindness only upon a believer.""**"
"13656","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports in elevated form that the Messenger ﷺ likened fitting beneficence toward the noble or religious to training only a pedigreed mount. > **""Beneficence is fitting only toward one of lineage or religion, just as training is fitting only for a pedigreed mount.""**"
"13657","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ described the early generations as best and the latter as worst in division and discord, and commanded the believer in Allah and the Last Day to meet death while dealing with people as he loves to be dealt with. > **""The first of this nation are its best and its last are its worst, differing and divided. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let death come to him while he deals with people as he loves to be dealt with.""**"
"13658","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ repeatedly affirmed the brotherhood of Muslims without wrong or abandonment, swore that no two persons bound in affection separate except through a sin newly committed by one of them, and enumerated six recognised duties toward a brother. > **""The Muslim is the brother of the Muslim; he does not wrong him nor abandon him. By the One in whose Hand is my soul, no two persons love one another and then separate except through a sin that one of them commits. Toward a Muslim upon his brother are six acts of kindness: he responds with mercy when he sneezes, visits him when ill, advises him in absence, bears witness with him and greets him when he meets him, answers him when he invites him, and follows him when he dies.""**"
"13659","A man of Bani Salit reports that he came upon the Prophet ﷺ amid a crowd and heard him affirm Muslim brotherhood without wrong or abandonment and locate God-consciousness in the heart, with Hammad adding that the Prophet ﷺ pointed with his hand to his chest. > **""The Muslim is the brother of the Muslim; he does not wrong him nor abandon him. God-consciousness is here.""**"
"13660","Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum reports from his father concerning a sea expedition in which the companions invited Abu Ayyub al-Ansari رضي الله عنه and his crew to luncheon, Abu Ayyub's acceptance despite his fast as a binding duty, his transmission of six obligatory rights of a Muslim toward his brother, and the ensuing incident in which a jesting man repeatedly blessed the man in charge of provisions until provoked to anger and insult, sought Abu Ayyub's judgment, was advised that whoever is not reformed by good is reformed by its opposite, reversed the formula to a curse that drew laughter and approval, and then blessed Abu Ayyub himself for the counsel. > **""A Muslim has six obligatory traits toward his Muslim brother, and whoever abandons one of them has abandoned an obligatory right: when he invites him, he answers him; when he meets him, he greets him; when he sneezes, he responds with mercy; when he falls ill, he visits him; when he dies, he accompanies his funeral; and when he seeks his counsel, he counsels him. Whoever is not reformed by good will be reformed by evil.""**"
"13661","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ assigned to the believer six traits owed to a fellow believer concerning greeting, response to sneezing, acceptance of invitation, visitation in illness, attendance at death, and counsel in absence. > **""A believer owes a believer six traits: he greets him when he meets him, responds with mercy when he sneezes, answers him when he invites him, visits him when he falls ill, attends him when he dies, and counsels him in his absence.""**"
"13662","In a version of the rights of a Muslim, the Messenger ﷺ is reported to have enjoined acceptance of an invitation even when it is merely for a trotter. > **""If he invites him, even over a trotter, he answers him.""**"
"13663","Wathilah ibn al-Asqa رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ declare a Muslim's blood, honour and wealth inviolable to another Muslim, affirm brotherhood without wrong or abandonment, point to the heart as the seat of God-consciousness, and deem contempt for a Muslim brother sufficient evil for a man. > **""A Muslim is inviolable to a Muslim: his blood, his honour, and his wealth. The Muslim is the brother of the Muslim; he does not wrong him nor abandon him. God-consciousness is here, and he pointed with his hand to the heart. It suffices a man as evil that he despise his Muslim brother.""**"
"13664","Ubaydullah ibn Ziyad al-Hadrami reports that Malik ibn Dinar met Salim ibn Abdullah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab riding a donkey with drooping ears, worn saddle and clothes, that upon inquiry Malik traced himself to Salim's household and freedmen, and that Salim transmitted from his father from the Messenger ﷺ the brotherhood that neither abandons nor betrays nor forgets in calamity, and the observation that the ruin of the best of Arabs and freedmen through mutual love or of the worst through mutual hatred leaves no alternative. > **""The Muslim is the brother of the Muslim; he does not abandon him, nor betray him, nor forget him in a calamity that befalls him. If the best of Arabs and freedmen perish while loving one another, they find no escape from it, and if the worst of the two parties perish while hating one another, they find no escape from it.""**"
"13665","Abdullah رضي الله عنه states six duties of kindness owed by a Muslim to a Muslim: greeting upon meeting, answering invitation, responding to sneezing, attending death, counselling in absence, and loving for him what one loves for oneself. > **""A Muslim owes a Muslim six things with kindness: he greets him when he meets him, answers him when he invites him, responds with mercy when he sneezes, attends him when he dies, counsels him in absence, and loves for him what he loves for himself.""**"
"13666","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that when the Prophet ﷺ asked about a man and one present claimed acquaintance yet knew neither his name nor his father's name, the Prophet ﷺ denied that this constituted knowledge until name, father's name and tribe are known, adding visitation in illness and following the funeral as its fruits. > **""Who knows him? What is his name? What is his father's name? This is not knowledge until you know his name, his father's name, and his tribe. If he falls ill, you visit him, and if he dies, you follow his funeral.""**"
"13667","Khalid ibn Abdullah al-Qasri reports from his father from his grandfather that the Prophet ﷺ instructed his grandfather Yazid ibn Asad رضي الله عنه to love for people what he loves for himself. > **""Love for people what you love for yourself.""**"
"13668","In a version also transmitted through Khalid from his father from his grandfather, the Messenger ﷺ is reported to have asked whether he loved Paradise and, upon his assent, commanded love for one's brother of what one loves for oneself. > **""Do you love Paradise? Love for your brother what you love for yourself.""**"
"13669","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger ﷺ promised removal from the Fire and entry into Paradise to one whom death overtakes while testifying that none is worthy of worship but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger ﷺ, and while dealing with people as he loves to be dealt with. > **""Whoever is pleased to be removed from the Fire and admitted into Paradise, let death come to him while he testifies that none is worthy of worship but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger, and while he deals with people as he loves to be dealt with.""**"
"13670","Abu Said, meaning al-Khudri رضي الله عنه, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that mercy withheld from people returns as mercy withheld. > **""Whoever does not show mercy to people will not be shown mercy.""**"
"13671","Abu Musa al-Ashari رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ condition faith upon mutual mercy, and when the people claimed mercy for themselves, the Prophet ﷺ explained that what is meant is not private compassion for a companion but mercy toward people generally. > **""You will not believe until you show mercy to one another. It is not the mercy of one of you to his companion, but mercy to people, mercy to the generality.""**"
"13672","Jarir رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ promise heavenly mercy in return for earthly mercy. > **""Show mercy to those on earth, and the One in heaven will show mercy to you.""**"
"13673","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ swore that Allah places His mercy only upon the merciful, and when the people claimed mercy for themselves, he clarified that what is intended is mercy to all people, not merely to one's companion. > **""By the One in whose Hand is my soul, Allah places His mercy only upon the merciful. It is not the mercy of one of you to his companion; it is mercy to all people.""**"
"13674","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ promised heavenly mercy in return for earthly mercy. > **""Show mercy to those on earth, and the One in heaven will show mercy to you.""**"
"13675","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the unmerciful are left without mercy. > **""Whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy.""**"
"13676","Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the unmerciful are left without mercy. > **""Whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy.""**"
"13677","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ deny divine mercy to one who withholds mercy from people. > **""Whoever does not show mercy to people, Allah will not show mercy to him.""**"
"13678","Muawiyah ibn Haydah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ denied divine mercy to one who withholds mercy from people. > **""Whoever does not show mercy to people, Allah will never show mercy to him.""**"
"13679","Al-Ashath ibn Qays رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ denied divine mercy to one who withholds mercy from Muslims. > **""Whoever does not show mercy to Muslims, Allah will never show mercy to him.""**"
"13680","Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the station of a believer among the faithful resembles the head to the body, sharing pain as the body shares the pain of the head. > **""The believer among the people of faith is like the head to the body: the believer feels pain for the people of faith just as the body feels pain for what afflicts the head.""**"
"13681","Bashir ibn Sad رضي الله عنه, a companion of the Messenger ﷺ, reports that the Messenger ﷺ likened the standing of believer to believer to that of head to body, in mutual participation of complaint between part and whole. > **""The standing of a believer to a believer is that of the head to the body: whenever the body complains, the head complains for it, and whenever the head complains, the rest of the body complains.""**"
"13682","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه and Abu Said رضي الله عنه report from the Prophet ﷺ that believers strengthen one another like the parts of a building. > **""The believer to the believer is like a building, one part of which strengthens another.""**"
"13683","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ defined his mission as the completion of righteous character. > **""I was sent only to perfect righteous character.""**"
"13684","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger ﷺ described his sending as bearing the completion of noble morals and the perfection of beautiful deeds. > **""Allah sent me with the completion of noble morals and the perfection of beautiful deeds.""**"
"13685","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ count noble morals among the deeds of Paradise. > **""Noble morals are among the deeds of Paradise.""**"
"13686","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ described Allah as Beautiful who loves beauty, loves lofty morals, and dislikes the base and petty. > **""Allah is Beautiful and loves beauty. He loves lofty morals and dislikes the base and petty.""**"
"13687","Sahl ibn Sad رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger ﷺ described Allah the Mighty and Majestic as Generous who loves the generous and loves high matters while disliking the petty, with the variant wording naming high morals. > **""Allah the Mighty and Majestic is Generous and loves the generous. He loves high matters and dislikes the petty. He loves high morals.""**"
"13688","Husayn ibn Ali رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger ﷺ declared Allah's love for high and noble matters and His dislike for the petty. > **""Allah loves high and noble matters and dislikes the petty.""**"
"13689","Uqbah ibn Amir, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that he afterwards met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and took hold of his hand, asking him to inform him of the most excellent deeds. The Prophet ﷺ enjoined upon him maintaining relations, giving, and turning away. > **""O Uqbah, join whoever cuts you off, give to whoever deprives you, and turn away from whoever wrongs you.""**"
"13690","This is an alternate wording of the same report, conveying the final injunction with the term pardon rather than turning away, without additional chain detail in the matn itself. > **""And pardon whoever wronged you.""**"
"13691","Ali, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Prophet ﷺ addressed him and offered to guide him to the noblest character in this world and the next, which he then defined through three acts of generosity toward those who withhold and wrong. > **""Shall I not guide you to the noblest morals of this world and the Hereafter? That you join whoever cuts you off, give to whoever deprives you, and pardon whoever wrongs you.""**"
"13692","Kab ibn Ujrah, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to guide his Companions to the best character of this world and the Hereafter, describing it through kindness toward the estranged, the oppressive, and the withholding. > **""Shall I not guide you to the best morals of this world and the Hereafter? One who joins whoever cuts him off, pardons whoever wrongs him, and gives to whoever deprives him.""**"
"13693","Muadh ibn Anas, radiya Allahu anhu, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a definition of the most excellent of excellences, expressed through maintaining ties, giving, and overlooking insult. > **""The best of excellences: that you join whoever cuts you off, give to whoever deprives you, and overlook whoever reviles you.""**"
"13694","Ubadah ibn al-Samit, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked his Companions whether he should guide them to what raises ranks, and upon their assent he listed forbearance, pardon, giving, and joining. > **""Shall I not guide you to what Allah raises ranks by? Show forbearance toward whoever acts ignorantly against you, pardon whoever wrongs you, give to whoever deprives you, and join whoever cuts you off.""**"
"13695","Ubadah, radiya Allahu anhu, also reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to inform his Companions of what ennobles their edifice and raises ranks, and upon their assent he named forbearance, joining, giving, and pardoning. > **""Shall I not inform you of what Allah Almighty ennobles the edifice by and raises ranks by? That you show forbearance toward whoever acts ignorantly against you, join whoever cuts you off, give to whoever deprives you, and pardon whoever wrongs you.""**"
"13696","Ubayy ibn Kab, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ linked desire for a lofty edifice and raised ranks to three deeds toward those who wrong, deprive, and sever. > **""Whoever delights that a lofty edifice be granted to him and ranks be raised for him, let him pardon whoever wronged him, give to whoever deprived him, and join whoever cut him off.""**"
"13697","Abu Hurayrah, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned three traits whose bearer receives an easy reckoning and entry into Paradise through mercy. When asked with reverent affection what they were, he named giving, joining, and pardoning, and when asked what reward follows such action, he promised Paradise. > **""You give to whoever deprived you, join whoever cut you off, and pardon whoever wronged you. Allah will admit you to Paradise.""**"
"13698","Abu Hurayrah, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that a man reviled Abu Bakr, radiya Allahu anhu, while the Prophet ﷺ sat by, marveling and smiling. When the abuse increased, Abu Bakr answered back in part, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ grew angry and rose. Abu Bakr followed and asked why his silence had met approval but his reply met anger. The Prophet ﷺ explained that an angel had been answering on his behalf until Shaytan intruded upon his reply, with whom he would not sit, and then addressed Abu Bakr with three certain truths about pardon bringing honor, giving for kinship bringing increase, and begging for increase bringing decrease. > **""There was an angel with you answering on your behalf, but when you answered back in part, Shaytan intervened, and I would not sit with Shaytan. O Abu Bakr, three are all true: no servant is wronged with an injustice and then overlooks it for Allah Almighty except that Allah strengthens his support through it, no man opens a door of giving intending kinship except that Allah increases him thereby in abundance, and no man opens a door of begging intending increase except that Allah increases him thereby in decrease.""**"
"13699","Saib ibn Abdillah, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that Uthman ibn Affan and Zuhayr brought him to the Prophet ﷺ and began praising him. The Prophet ﷺ said he already knew him as his companion in the pre-Islamic age. Saib affirmed what an excellent companion he had been. The Prophet ﷺ then commanded him to preserve his former noble conduct in Islam through hospitality, care for the orphan, and goodness to the neighbor. > **""O Saib, look to the morals you practiced in the pre-Islamic age and practice them in Islam: honor the guest, honor the orphan, and do good to your neighbor.""**"
"13700","Umm Salamah, radiya Allahu anha, reports that the Prophet ﷺ stated that one lacking even one of three qualities will not profit from his deeds, namely restraining piety, restraining forbearance, and congenial character. She also reports that he stated that one possessing even one of three other qualities will be wed to maidens of Paradise, namely faithful return of a coveted hidden trust, pardon of a killer, and recitation of the chapter of Sincerity after every prayer. > **""Whoever lacks even one of three will gain nothing from his deeds: piety restraining him from disobedience to Allah, forbearance by which he restrains a fool, or character with which he lives among people. Whoever possesses even one of three, Allah will wed him among the wide-eyed maidens: one with a hidden coveted trust who discharges it from fear of Allah, or a man who pardons his killer, or a man who recites Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad after every prayer.""**"
"13701","Abu Umamah, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ connected pardon at the moment of power with divine pardon on the day of hardship. > **""Whoever pardons while having power, Allah will pardon him on the Day of Hardship.""**"
"13702","Anas, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described one who walks out for the need of his Muslim brother as earning a good deed for every step until his return, emerging sinless as a newborn if the need is fulfilled, and entering Paradise without reckoning if he perishes along the way. > **""Whoever walks to the need of his Muslim brother, Allah writes for him a good deed for every step he takes until he returns whence he parted. If his need is fulfilled, he emerges from his sins as on the day his mother bore him, and if he perishes along the way, such a perished one enters Paradise without reckoning.""**"
"13703","Anas, radiya Allahu anhu, also reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised a multiplied reward for every step taken in service of a Muslim brother's need. > **""Whoever walks for the need of his Muslim brother, Allah writes for him seventy good deeds for every step he takes.""**"
"13704","Anas ibn Malik, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that kindness to a believer or exertion for any of his needs, small or great, makes it binding upon Allah to provide him service from the attendants of Paradise. > **""Whoever treats a believer gently or exerts himself in any of his needs, whether small or great, it is binding upon Allah to provide him service from the attendants of Paradise.""**"
"13705","Anas ibn Malik, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised seventy-three good deeds to one who rescues the anguished, with one rectifying his worldly and otherworldly affairs and seventy-two elevating his ranks. > **""Whoever rescues one in anguish, Allah writes for him seventy-three good deeds, by one of which Allah rectifies for him his affairs of this world and the next, and seventy-two are in ranks.""**"
"13706","Anas, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described creation as the dependents of Allah and identified the most beloved among them as the most beneficial to those dependents. > **""Creation are the dependents of Allah, and the most beloved of them to Allah are the most beneficial to His dependents.""**"
"13707","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the whole of creation as dependents of Allah and identified the most beloved as the most beneficial to them. > **""All creation are dependents of Allah, and the most beloved of creation to Allah are the most beneficial to His dependents.""**"
"13708","Ibn Umar, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ which people and which deeds are most beloved to Allah. The Prophet ﷺ answered that the most beloved are the most beneficial, and named bringing joy, relieving distress, settling debt, and repelling hunger. He then declared walking with a brother for his need dearer than a month of retreat in his mosque at Madinah, and described the rewards of curbing rage, swallowing controllable anger, and accompanying a brother until his need is secured, culminating in firm footing on the day feet slip. > **""The most beloved of people to Allah Almighty are the most beneficial to people, and the most beloved of deeds to Allah are joy you bring to a Muslim, or distress you remove from him, or debt you settle for him, or hunger you repel from him. That I walk with a brother of mine for his need is dearer to me than that I observe retreat in this mosque of mine in Madinah for a month. Whoever restrains his rage, Allah will veil his faults, and whoever swallows his anger though able to vent it, Allah will fill his heart with ease on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever walks with his brother for his need until it is secured for him, Allah will make his foot firm on the day feet slip.""**"
"13709","Aishah, radiya Allahu anha, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised divine aid upon the Bridge when feet slip to one who conveys his Muslim brother's case to an authority for benefit or relief of hardship. > **""Whoever serves as a link for his Muslim brother to one in authority for attaining goodness or easing hardship, Allah will aid him in crossing the Bridge when feet slip.""**"
"13710","Ibn Umar, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described certain of Allah's creation as fashioned for the needs of people, to whom people hasten, and who remain secure from Allah's punishment. > **""Allah has a creation whom He created for the needs of people; people hasten to them for their needs; those are the ones secure from the punishment of Allah.""**"
"13711","Abu al-Darda, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised elevation in the highest ranks of Paradise to one who conveys his Muslim brother's case to an authority for goodness or joy. > **""Whoever serves as a link for his Muslim brother to one in authority for attaining goodness or bringing joy, Allah will raise him in the highest ranks of Paradise.""**"
"13712","Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Allah Almighty declared Himself the Creator of good and evil, announcing glad tidings for one through whose hands good is decreed and woe for one through whose hands evil is decreed. > **""I created good and evil, so glad tidings to one through whose hands I decreed good, and woe to one through whose hands I decreed evil.""**"
"13713","Abdullah ibn Amr, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that Allah confirms blessings with certain peoples while they attend to the needs of others without weariness, then transfers them when they grow weary. > **""Allah has blessings with certain peoples which He confirms with them while they attend to the needs of people, so long as they do not grow weary; when they grow weary, He transfers them to others.""**"
"13714","Ibn Umar, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that Allah singles out certain peoples with blessings for the benefit of His servants, retaining them while they spend freely, then removing and redirecting them when they withhold. > **""Allah has peoples whom He singled out with blessings for the benefit of His servants; He confirms them therein while they spend them, but when they withhold them, He strips them from them and transfers them to others.""**"
"13715","Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that a servant abundantly blessed who then grows vexed when people's needs are directed to him exposes that blessing to removal. > **""There is no servant upon whom Allah bestows a blessing and completes it for him, then makes the needs of people turn to him, and he grows vexed, except that he exposes that blessing to removal.""**"
"13716","Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that walking for a brother's need surpasses ten years of retreat, and that a single day of retreat seeking Allah's Face places three vast trenches between the observer and the Fire, each vaster than the distance between the horizons. > **""Whoever walks for the need of his brother is better for him than ten years of retreat, and whoever observes retreat for a day seeking the Face of Allah, Allah places between him and the Fire three trenches, each trench further than the distance between the two horizons.""**"
"13717","Abu Hurayrah, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised one who relieves a Muslim's distress two branches of light upon the Bridge on the Day of Resurrection, whose illumination benefits a multitude known only to the Lord of Might. > **""Whoever relieves a Muslim of distress, Allah Almighty will appoint for him on the Day of Resurrection two branches of light upon the Bridge, by whose light a world will be illuminated whom none can count but the Lord of Might.""**"
"13718","Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ identified the most beloved deed after the obligations as bringing joy to a Muslim. > **""The most beloved of deeds to Allah Almighty after the obligations is bringing joy to a Muslim.""**"
"13719","Hasan ibn Ali, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that bringing joy to one's Muslim brother ranks among the deeds that necessitate forgiveness. > **""Among the necessitators of forgiveness is bringing joy to your Muslim brother.""**"
"13720","Aishah, radiya Allahu anha, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that one who brings joy to a Muslim household will be granted no reward less than Paradise. > **""Whoever brings joy to a household among the Muslims, Allah will not accept for him a reward less than Paradise.""**"
"13721","Anas ibn Malik, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that one who meets his Muslim brother with what Allah loves in order to delight him will himself be delighted by Allah on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Whoever meets his Muslim brother with what Allah loves, to delight him thereby, Allah Almighty will delight him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"13722","Kab ibn Ujrah, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised reciprocal easing and veiling to one who relieves a believer's distress, veils his faults, and grants him relief. > **""Whoever eases a distress of a believer, Allah will ease his distress on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever veils the fault of a believer, Allah will veil his fault, and whoever grants relief to a believer, Allah will grant him relief.""**"
"13723","Zayd ibn Thabit, radiya Allahu anhu, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that divine aid accompanies the servant for as long as he attends to his brother's need. > **""Allah remains in the need of the servant while he remains in the need of his brother.""**"
"13724","Abu Hurayrah, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised reciprocal relief on the Day of Resurrection to one who relieves a worldly distress, reciprocal veiling to one who veils a Muslim, and continuous divine aid to one engaged in his brother's need. > **""Whoever relieves a Muslim of a distress of this world, Allah will relieve him of a distress of the Day of Resurrection, and whoever veils the fault of a Muslim, Allah will veil his fault on the Day of Resurrection, and Allah remains in the need of the servant while the servant remains in the need of his brother.""**"
"13725","Abdullah ibn Amr and Abu Hurayrah, radiya Allahu anhum, report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described one who walks for his Muslim brother's need as shaded by seventy-five thousand angels supplicating for him, wading in mercy until completion, and then credited with pilgrimage and lesser pilgrimage. The narrator notes that the full report was cited and had earlier appeared in the funerals chapter on visiting the sick. > **""Whoever walks for the need of his Muslim brother, Allah Almighty will shade him with seventy-five thousand angels supplicating for him, and he will continue wading in mercy until he finishes; when he finishes, Allah will write for him a pilgrimage and a lesser pilgrimage.""**"
"13726","Muawiyah ibn Haydah, radiya Allahu anhu, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that secret charity extinguishes the Lord's anger, deeds of kindness guard against evil deaths, kinship lengthens life and removes poverty, and that the formula of no power save through Allah should be repeated often as a treasure of Paradise containing cure for ninety-nine ailments, the least being anxiety. > **""Secret charity extinguishes the anger of the Lord, and deeds of kindness guard against evil deaths, and joining kinship increases lifespan and removes poverty. Increase in saying there is no power nor strength except through Allah, for it is a treasure among the treasures of Paradise, and therein is cure for ninety-nine ailments, the least of them anxiety.""**"
"13727","Samurah ibn Jundub, radiya Allahu anhu, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the best charity is that of the tongue. When asked what that meant, he explained it as intercession that frees a captive, prevents bloodshed, draws benefit to a brother, and repels harm from him. > **""The best charity is that of the tongue. Intercession by which a captive is freed, blood is spared, goodness and kindness are drawn to your brother, and harm is repelled from him.""**"
"13728","Sahl ibn Sad, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that a woman came to the Prophet ﷺ while his Companions were present, circled seeking a place, and found none. A man perceived her need, rose, and she sat and fulfilled her purpose before departing. The Prophet ﷺ asked the man whether he knew her, and upon his denial thrice invoked mercy upon him for having shown her mercy. > **""Do you know her? You showed her mercy, may Allah have mercy on you.""**"
"13729","Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, instructs that one should not present a need to a blind man nor present it by night, but should face the man directly since modesty resides in the eyes, and should seek it early in the morning, citing the Prophet's ﷺ supplication for blessing in the early hours of his community. > **""O Allah, bless my community in its early hours.""**"
"13730","Jabir, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded seeking goodness among those of handsome countenance. > **""Seek goodness among those of handsome faces.""**"
"13731","Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described one granted a handsome face, a handsome name, and a placement free of disgrace as among Allah's elite creation. Ibn Abbas then cited a poet's verse affirming the Prophetic condition of seeking goodness among bright faces. > **""Whoever is granted by Allah a handsome face, a handsome name, and placement in a station free of disgrace, he is among Allah's elite from His creation. Seek goodness among those of bright faces.""**"
"13732","Mujahid reports from Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, in what he believes to be a raised report, the command to seek goodness among those of handsome faces. > **""Seek goodness among those of handsome faces.""**"
"13733","Abu Hurayrah, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded directing needs toward those of handsome faces. > **""Seek needs among those of handsome faces.""**"
"13734","Yazid ibn Khusayfah reports from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded seeking goodness among those of handsome faces. > **""Seek goodness among those of handsome faces.""**"
"13735","Aishah, radiya Allahu anha, reports that the Prophet ﷺ commanded seeking goodness among those of handsome faces. > **""Seek goodness among those of handsome faces.""**"
"13736","Abu Said al-Khudri, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded seeking goodness from the merciful of his community and living under their shelter, forbidding its pursuit from the hard-hearted, who await divine displeasure. > **""Seek goodness from the merciful of my community and live under their shelter, and do not seek it from the hard-hearted, for they await My displeasure.""**"
"13737","Muadh ibn Jabal, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded seeking help in fulfilling needs through concealment, since every recipient of blessing is envied. > **""Seek help in fulfilling your needs through concealment, for everyone endowed with blessing is envied.""**"
"13738","Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that people of blessings have enviers against whom caution is due. > **""Those endowed with blessings have enviers, so beware of them.""**"
"13739","Anas, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded keeping good company with the blessings of Allah and not repelling them, since they rarely return once they depart from a people. > **""Honor the neighborhood of Allah's blessings and do not repel them, for rarely do they depart from a people and then return to them.""**"
"13740","Dirar ibn al-Azwar, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that they gifted a milch she-camel to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he began milking her. When he pressed hard to exhaust the milk, the Prophet ﷺ forbade him and commanded leaving what invites further milk, with a variant adding that some be left for him. > **""Do not do so; leave what invites the milk. Leave the callers of milk, and leave some for me.""**"
"13741","Nuqadah, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him to find a milch and rideable she-camel without a newborn. After searching the herds without success, he found such a camel among his cousin's livestock and brought her. The Prophet ﷺ then commanded him to spare what sustains the flow of milk. > **""O Nuqadah, seek for me a milch and rideable she-camel, without having newly given birth. O Nuqadah, spare what sustains the milk.""**"
"13742","Nuqadah, radiya Allahu anhu, reports in another wording that his uncle sent a milch camel to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who told him to milk her. After he milked, the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to leave what sustains the milk, so he left the udders standing without stripping all the milk. > **""O Nuqadah, leave what sustains the milk.""**"
"13743","Abdullah ibn Amr, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed a man milking a sheep and instructed him by name to leave something for its young, since it is among the most dutiful of animals. > **""O so-and-so, when you milk, leave something for its young, for it is among the most dutiful of animals.""**"
"13744","Sawadah ibn al-Rabi, radiya Allahu anhu, reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked of him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ granted him a herd of camels and instructed him to command his household upon returning to feed their young stock well and to trim their nails so as not to wound the udders of their livestock when milking. > **""When you return to your household, command them to feed their young stock well, and command them to trim their nails, so they do not wound the udders of their livestock with them when they milk.""**"
"13745","Al-Zubayr reports that he asked Jabir, radiya Allahu anhu, whether he had seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying while mounted, and he affirmed it. A man who had purchased a she-camel then came seeking the Prophet's ﷺ supplication for it, spoke to him while he prayed, and the Prophet ﷺ remained silent before supplicating for him after the salutation. > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah praying while mounted, and he supplicated for the owner of the she-camel after completing his prayer.""**"
"13746","Abdullah ibn Amr, radiya Allahu anhuma, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the noon prayer and found a hobbled she-camel, asking for its owner without response. He entered the mosque and prayed until finished, found the mount as before, and asked again. Its owner answered that it was his, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ warned him to fear Allah regarding it by either feeding it properly or releasing it to graze for itself. > **""Where is the owner of this mount? Do you not fear Allah Almighty regarding her? Either feed her properly, or release her so she may graze for herself.""**"
